/* Lessons for Microsoft Certified: Azure AI Fundamentals (AI-900): one per plan topic, matched by exact topic text.
   How to write and check them: docs/LESSON_GUIDE.md */
CertHub.addLessons("ai-900", [
 {
  t: "What AI is: models that learn patterns from data, and how that differs from rule-based software",
  body: [
   "Artificial intelligence (AI) is software that imitates human abilities such as seeing, understanding language, making predictions and creating content. On AI-900 you are not expected to build AI from scratch. You are expected to recognize what kind of problem AI can solve, which Azure service fits, and what responsible use looks like. Almost everything in modern AI rests on one idea: a model that has learned patterns from data.",
   "Traditional software is rule-based. A developer writes explicit logic, such as 'if the order total is over 100, apply free shipping'. That works when the rules are known and stable. It breaks down for tasks where nobody can write the rules, such as telling a cat from a dog in a photo or deciding whether a review sounds angry. There are too many variations to list.",
   "Machine learning (ML) turns this around. Instead of writing the rules, you give an algorithm many examples of inputs and, usually, the correct outputs. Training adjusts the model's internal values until its outputs match the examples as closely as possible. The result is a model: a function that takes new input and returns a prediction. Using the trained model on new data is called inferencing (or inference).",
   "Because a model learned from examples, its output is a probability-based best guess, not a guaranteed truth. A vision model might say 'dog, 94% confidence'. A language model might write a fluent paragraph that contains a mistake. This is why AI solutions need testing, human oversight and the responsible AI principles you will study later in this domain. It is also why the quality and representativeness of training data matter so much: a model can only learn patterns that are present in its data, including unwanted ones such as bias.",
   "Most AI you will meet on the exam is delivered as a service. Azure AI services (Vision, Language, Speech, Translator, Document Intelligence, Content Safety and others) expose pretrained models through an API, so you send an image or text and get results back. Azure Machine Learning is for training your own models on your own data. Microsoft Foundry brings together generative AI models, agents and AI services in one portal. Knowing which of these to pick for a scenario is a large part of AI-900."
  ],
  terms: [["Model", "A function learned from data that takes new input and returns a prediction, label, score or generated content."], ["Training", "The process of adjusting a model's internal values using example data until its outputs match the examples well."], ["Inference", "Using a trained model to make predictions on new data; also called inferencing or scoring."], ["Rule-based software", "Software whose behavior comes from explicit logic written by a developer rather than from patterns learned from data."]],
  example: "A spam filter written with rules might block any email containing the word 'prize'. Spammers quickly change the wording. A machine learning spam filter is trained on thousands of emails already marked spam or not spam, learns hundreds of subtle signals, and keeps improving when it is retrained on newly reported messages.",
  tip: "If a scenario says the outcome is hard to express as rules and there is plenty of historical example data, the answer involves machine learning. Remember that model outputs are probabilistic, so answers claiming AI is always correct are wrong.",
  check: [["What is the difference between training and inference?", "Training builds the model from example data; inference uses the trained model to make predictions on new data."], ["Why can two similar inputs to an AI model give a wrong answer for one of them?", "A model gives a probability-based prediction learned from its training data, not a guaranteed result, so unusual or poorly represented inputs can be misclassified."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Common AI workloads: prediction and forecasting, anomaly detection, computer vision, NLP, document processing and generative AI",
  body: [
   "A workload is a category of problem that AI solves. The first skill AI-900 measures is recognizing the workload behind a business scenario, because once you know the workload you can choose the technique and the Azure service. Exam questions usually describe a need in plain business language and ask which workload it is.",
   "Prediction and forecasting use historical data to estimate an unknown or future value: next month's sales, the price a house will sell for, or whether a customer will cancel a subscription. These are classic machine learning tasks (regression for numbers, classification for categories). Anomaly detection learns what normal looks like and flags unusual events, such as a fraudulent card transaction, a sudden spike in server errors or a sensor reading that suggests a machine is about to fail.",
   "Computer vision interprets images and video: classifying photos, detecting and locating objects, reading text in images (optical character recognition, OCR) and analyzing faces. Natural language processing (NLP) works with written language: detecting the language, judging sentiment, extracting key phrases and entities, summarizing and translating. Speech is closely related: converting speech to text and text to speech. Conversational AI combines language and speech to build bots and assistants that hold a dialog.",
   "Document processing (often called document intelligence) extracts structured data from forms and documents such as invoices, receipts and ID cards: not just the text, but which value is the total and which is the due date. Knowledge mining goes further, extracting information from large volumes of unstructured content and building a searchable index, so people can find insights across thousands of files.",
   "Generative AI creates new content in response to a prompt: text, code, images and more. It powers copilots and chat assistants that draft, summarize and answer questions, and agents that can also take actions. Unlike the other workloads, which analyze existing data, generative AI produces something that did not exist before, which brings its own risks such as fabricated answers.",
   "Real solutions often combine workloads. A claims app might use OCR to read a form, entity recognition to find the policy number, anomaly detection to flag suspicious claims and a generative model to draft a letter. On the exam, focus on the main need described in the question."
  ],
  terms: [["Workload", "A category of problem that AI addresses, such as computer vision, NLP or anomaly detection."], ["Anomaly detection", "Identifying data points or events that differ significantly from the normal pattern."], ["Knowledge mining", "Extracting information from large amounts of unstructured content and making it searchable."], ["Generative AI", "AI that creates new content, such as text, code or images, from a prompt."]],
  example: "An airline uses forecasting to predict passenger numbers per flight, anomaly detection to spot unusual engine sensor data, computer vision to check baggage images, NLP to analyze complaint emails, and a generative AI assistant to help agents draft rebooking messages.",
  tip: "Look for the verb in the scenario: predict or estimate points to ML prediction, flag unusual points to anomaly detection, see or read an image points to computer vision, understand text points to NLP, extract fields from forms points to document processing, and create or draft points to generative AI.",
  check: [["A factory wants alerts when vibration readings on a motor look unusual. Which workload is this?", "Anomaly detection, because it flags readings that differ from the normal pattern."], ["What distinguishes generative AI from the other workloads?", "It creates new content from a prompt, whereas the others analyze or extract information from existing data."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Computer vision workloads: image classification, object detection, optical character recognition and facial analysis",
  body: [
   "Computer vision is the area of AI that lets software interpret images and video. To a computer an image is just a grid of numbers (pixel values), so vision models learn which patterns of pixels correspond to meaningful things such as a cat, a stop sign or the letter A. Domain 3 covers the Azure services in detail; here you need to recognize the main vision workloads in a scenario.",
   "Image classification assigns a label to a whole image: this photo shows a bicycle, this X-ray is normal, this leaf has a disease. The output is one label (or a list of labels with confidence scores) for the image as a whole. It does not tell you where in the image the thing is or how many there are.",
   "Object detection finds individual objects and returns a label and a bounding box (the coordinates of a rectangle) for each one. Use it when you need to count or locate things: how many cars are in the car park, where each person is standing, which shelf slots are empty. Semantic segmentation goes further and labels every pixel, giving the exact outline of each object.",
   "Optical character recognition (OCR) detects and extracts printed or handwritten text from images and scanned documents: street signs, receipts, whiteboards, number plates. OCR returns the text and where it appears. If you need to know what the text means, such as which number is the total on an invoice, you combine OCR with document processing.",
   "Facial analysis covers face detection (finding faces and their location), face attribute analysis (such as head pose, glasses or blur) and face recognition (verifying that two images show the same person, or identifying a person from a group). Recognition is sensitive: Microsoft restricts identification and verification under its Limited Access policy and has retired features that inferred emotion, gender or age. Expect responsible AI questions around face scenarios.",
   "Image analysis services can also generate captions and tags that describe an image in words. These descriptions support accessibility (alt text for screen readers), content moderation and searching photo libraries."
  ],
  terms: [["Image classification", "Assigning one or more labels to an image as a whole, without locating objects."], ["Object detection", "Finding objects in an image and returning a label and bounding box for each."], ["Optical character recognition (OCR)", "Extracting printed or handwritten text from images or scanned documents."], ["Bounding box", "The coordinates of a rectangle that marks where an object or word appears in an image."]],
  example: "A supermarket installs shelf cameras. Object detection finds each product and its position so staff know which slots are empty, OCR reads the price labels to check they match the system, and image classification flags whether each shelf photo is 'tidy' or 'needs attention'.",
  tip: "Count and locate means object detection; one label for the whole image means classification; read text means OCR. If a question mentions identifying who a person is, expect Limited Access to be part of the correct answer.",
  check: [["A wildlife project needs to count how many deer appear in each trail-camera photo. Classification or object detection?", "Object detection, because it finds and locates each deer; classification would only say whether deer are present."], ["What does OCR return besides the text itself?", "The location of the text, usually as lines and words with bounding boxes."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Natural language processing and speech workloads: text analysis, translation, speech recognition and conversational AI",
  body: [
   "Natural language processing (NLP) is the area of AI that works with human language. It lets software make sense of emails, reviews, chat messages and documents, and respond in language people understand. Speech workloads extend this to spoken language. Domain 4 covers the Azure services; this lesson maps the workloads you will see in scenarios.",
   "Text analysis extracts information from written text. Typical tasks include language detection (which language is this?), sentiment analysis (is this review positive, negative or neutral?), key phrase extraction (what are the main talking points?), entity recognition (which people, places, organizations and dates are mentioned?) and summarization (give me the gist in a few sentences). These are analysis tasks: they return facts about the text rather than new content.",
   "Translation converts text or speech from one language to another. Modern translation uses neural models that translate whole sentences in context, rather than word by word, so idioms and word order come out more naturally. Organizations use it to localize product pages, support customers in their own language and make documents available across regions.",
   "Speech recognition (speech to text) converts spoken audio into text, for captions, meeting transcripts and voice commands. Speech synthesis (text to speech) does the opposite, producing natural-sounding audio from text for voice assistants, audiobooks and accessibility. Speech translation combines recognition and translation so a speaker in one language can be understood in another in near real time.",
   "Conversational AI builds bots and assistants that hold a dialog with users. A traditional bot needs to understand what the user wants (the intent), pull out details such as a date or city (entities) and respond, possibly by looking up an answer in a knowledge base of FAQs. Generative AI assistants are a newer form of conversational AI that write responses with a large language model. Both may use speech so users can talk rather than type.",
   "When you read a scenario, decide whether the input is text or audio, and whether the goal is to analyze, translate, answer or converse. That decision usually identifies the workload and narrows the service to Azure AI Language, Azure AI Speech or Azure AI Translator."
  ],
  terms: [["Natural language processing (NLP)", "AI that analyzes, understands and generates human language in text form."], ["Sentiment analysis", "Determining whether text expresses a positive, negative, neutral or mixed opinion."], ["Speech recognition", "Converting spoken audio into text; also called speech to text."], ["Conversational AI", "Software such as bots and assistants that interact with users through natural dialog."]],
  example: "A telecom company transcribes support calls with speech to text, runs sentiment analysis on the transcripts to find unhappy customers, translates messages from customers abroad, and offers a voice bot that answers routine billing questions and reads the answers aloud with text to speech.",
  tip: "Separate the direction of speech tasks: speech to text makes transcripts and captions; text to speech makes audio. Analysis tasks (sentiment, key phrases, entities) never generate new text.",
  check: [["A company wants subtitles for its training videos. Which workload is that?", "Speech recognition (speech to text), because it converts the spoken audio into text."], ["What two things must a traditional bot extract from a user's message to act on it?", "The intent (what the user wants) and the entities (details such as a date, place or product)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Document processing and knowledge mining workloads: extracting fields from forms and making content searchable",
  body: [
   "Organizations run on documents: invoices, receipts, purchase orders, tax forms, contracts, ID cards and medical records. Much of that information arrives as scans, photos or PDFs, and people spend hours typing it into systems. Document processing uses AI to read these documents and pull out the data automatically. Knowledge mining deals with the bigger problem of finding information across huge collections of content.",
   "Document processing, also called document intelligence, goes beyond OCR. OCR returns all the text on the page. Document processing understands structure and meaning: it returns key-value pairs (Invoice number: 4471), tables with rows and columns, checkboxes (selected or not) and named fields such as vendor name, total and due date, each with a confidence score. The output is structured data that can flow straight into an accounting or HR system.",
   "Prebuilt models handle common document types such as invoices, receipts, identity documents and business cards without any training. For documents unique to your organization, custom models learn your layout from a small set of labeled samples. In Azure this workload is delivered by Azure AI Document Intelligence, which you will meet in domain 3.",
   "Knowledge mining applies AI to large volumes of mostly unstructured content (documents, images, emails, web pages) to extract information and build a searchable index. A pipeline typically reads each file, runs OCR on images, extracts key phrases, entities and language, perhaps translates, and stores the enriched results in a search index. Users can then search and filter across everything, for example 'all contracts with Fabrikam that mention a termination clause'.",
   "In Azure, Azure AI Search provides the indexing and search, and AI enrichment (skills that call Azure AI services) adds the extracted information during indexing. The same kind of index, especially one that stores vector embeddings, is also what retrieval augmented generation (RAG) uses to ground generative AI answers in an organization's data, which you will study in domain 5.",
   "On the exam, the distinction is scope and output. Extract specific fields from a known kind of form: document processing. Make a large, varied body of content searchable and discoverable: knowledge mining."
  ],
  terms: [["Document intelligence", "AI that extracts text, key-value pairs, tables and named fields from documents into structured data."], ["Key-value pair", "A label and its value found in a document, such as 'Due date' and '30 June'."], ["Knowledge mining", "Extracting information from large volumes of unstructured content and building a searchable index."], ["Search index", "A store of processed content and extracted fields that users or apps can query quickly."]],
  example: "An insurer receives 5,000 claim forms a week. Document Intelligence extracts the policy number, date and amount from each form into the claims system. Separately, it indexes ten years of claim files, adjuster notes and photos with Azure AI Search so investigators can search for patterns across all past claims.",
  tip: "OCR alone gives you text; document processing gives you fields and tables; knowledge mining gives you a searchable index across many documents. Choose the one that matches the output the scenario wants.",
  check: [["A company wants the total and tax amount from every receipt employees upload. Which workload is this?", "Document processing, because it extracts specific named fields from a known document type."], ["Which Azure service provides the search index in a knowledge mining solution?", "Azure AI Search, with AI enrichment adding extracted information during indexing."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Generative AI workloads: creating text, code and images from prompts, copilots and agents",
  body: [
   "Generative AI is AI that creates new content. You give it a prompt, a natural language instruction or question, and it returns text, code, an image or other output that did not exist before. It is the fastest-growing area of AI and carries the largest weight on AI-900 (domain 5), so this lesson introduces the vocabulary.",
   "Most generative AI apps are built on large language models (LLMs), which are trained on vast amounts of text and generate responses one token at a time. With the right prompt the same model can draft an email, summarize a report, answer a question, translate, classify text or write and explain code. Image generation models create pictures from a text description. Multimodal models can take images as input as well as text.",
   "A copilot is a generative AI assistant built into an application to help people with their work: drafting documents in a word processor, summarizing a meeting, writing a formula in a spreadsheet or suggesting code in an editor. The name signals the intended relationship: the user remains the pilot, reviewing and deciding, while the AI assists.",
   "An agent goes a step further. It combines a model with instructions, knowledge and tools, such as the ability to search a knowledge base, run code or call a business API, so it can work toward a goal and take actions, sometimes over several steps. A travel agent bot that checks flight availability, books a seat and emails the confirmation is an agent; a bot that only writes a description of a flight is not.",
   "Generative AI brings specific risks. Models can produce fluent but false statements (often called hallucinations), reflect biases in their training data, generate harmful content or be manipulated by crafted prompts. Organizations reduce these risks by grounding the model in trusted data, using content filters, writing clear system messages, keeping humans in the loop and testing thoroughly. You will study these mitigations in detail later.",
   "On the exam, generative AI is the answer when the scenario asks to create, draft, write, rewrite, summarize in natural language or converse freely. If it asks to extract, classify with fixed labels or detect, a traditional AI service may be the more precise fit."
  ],
  terms: [["Prompt", "The instruction, question or context given to a generative AI model to produce a response."], ["Large language model (LLM)", "A very large neural network trained on text that generates language by predicting tokens."], ["Copilot", "A generative AI assistant embedded in an app that helps a user with tasks while the user stays in control."], ["Agent", "A generative AI solution that combines a model with instructions, knowledge and tools so it can take actions toward a goal."]],
  example: "A software company gives its support engineers a copilot inside the ticketing system. It summarizes long ticket histories, drafts replies based on the knowledge base, and, as an agent with the right tools, can look up a customer's licence status and reset a trial period after the engineer approves.",
  tip: "Copilot: assists a user inside an app. Agent: can also use tools and take actions. Both are generative AI. Watch for the word 'create' or 'draft' as the signal for a generative AI workload.",
  check: [["What makes an agent different from a basic chat assistant?", "An agent can use tools and knowledge to take actions toward a goal, not just reply with text."], ["Name two risks specific to generative AI output.", "Fabricated but plausible statements (hallucinations) and harmful or biased content; manipulation through crafted prompts is another."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Responsible AI principles: fairness, and reliability and safety",
  body: [
   "AI systems make or influence decisions that affect people: who gets a loan, which job applicants are shortlisted, how a car brakes. Microsoft groups its approach to responsible AI into six principles: fairness; reliability and safety; privacy and security; inclusiveness; transparency; and accountability. AI-900 tests whether you can match a scenario to the right principle, so this and the next two lessons take them two at a time.",
   "Fairness means AI systems should treat all people fairly. Two people with similar qualifications should get similar outcomes regardless of gender, ethnicity, age or other characteristics that should not matter. Unfairness usually comes from data: if historical hiring data reflects past bias, a model trained on it learns that bias. It can also creep in through proxies, such as postcode standing in for ethnicity.",
   "To support fairness, teams check whether training data represents all relevant groups, compare model performance and outcomes across groups, remove or reduce the influence of inappropriate features and involve diverse reviewers. Azure Machine Learning's Responsible AI dashboard includes fairness assessment that shows how metrics such as error rate or selection rate differ between groups.",
   "Reliability and safety means AI systems should perform reliably and safely, as intended, including in conditions they were not specifically built for. A model that works in the lab but fails on rainy days, unusual accents or rare medical cases is not reliable. For systems that act in the physical world, such as autonomous vehicles or medical devices, failure can harm people.",
   "Supporting reliability and safety involves rigorous testing with realistic and edge-case data, defining acceptable performance thresholds before release, monitoring the model in production for drift (performance getting worse as real-world data changes), and designing safe fallbacks, such as handing control to a human when the model's confidence is low. For generative AI it includes safety systems that block harmful output.",
   "These two principles are easy to confuse with others. Fairness is about equal treatment of groups. Inclusiveness, covered next, is about designing so everyone can use and benefit from the system. Reliability and safety is about the system working correctly and not causing harm, even when things go wrong."
  ],
  terms: [["Fairness", "The principle that AI should treat all people fairly and not give different outcomes to similar people based on irrelevant characteristics."], ["Reliability and safety", "The principle that AI should perform consistently and safely as intended, including in unexpected conditions."], ["Bias", "A systematic skew in data or model behavior that leads to unfair outcomes for some groups."], ["Model drift", "A decline in a model's performance over time as real-world data changes from the training data."]],
  example: "A bank tests its credit model and finds that applicants under 25 with the same income and repayment history as older applicants are approved far less often. It retrains with more representative data and adds fairness checks before each release (fairness). It also sends low-confidence decisions to a human underwriter and monitors approval accuracy every month (reliability and safety).",
  tip: "Unequal outcomes for groups points to fairness. Testing edge cases, handling failures safely or monitoring for drift points to reliability and safety.",
  check: [["A face detection system performs worse on darker skin tones. Which principle is most directly affected?", "Fairness, because one group gets worse outcomes than others."], ["A self-driving shuttle hands control to an operator when its sensors are obscured. Which principle does this show?", "Reliability and safety, because the system fails safely under unexpected conditions."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Responsible AI principles: privacy and security, and inclusiveness",
  body: [
   "AI systems depend on data, often large amounts of personal data, and they increasingly shape how people access services. The principles of privacy and security and of inclusiveness address two sides of this: protecting the people whose data is used, and making sure the system works for everyone who needs it.",
   "Privacy and security means AI systems should be secure and respect privacy. Training data may include names, health records, purchase histories or voice recordings. Organizations must follow privacy laws, collect only what they need, get appropriate consent, and protect data with access controls and encryption. The model itself needs protecting too: a trained model or its outputs can sometimes leak details of the data it was trained on, and attackers may try to manipulate inputs.",
   "Practical measures include removing or masking personal information before training (for example with PII detection), encrypting data at rest and in transit, restricting who can access data and models, keeping audit logs, and letting people know how their data is used. For generative AI apps, it also means not sending confidential data to services that would store or reuse it inappropriately and controlling what documents a grounded chatbot can retrieve for each user. Azure AI services process customer data under Microsoft's data protection commitments; for example, Microsoft states that prompts and completions sent to Azure OpenAI are not used to train the underlying models.",
   "Inclusiveness means AI systems should empower everyone and engage people. It is about who can use and benefit from the system. A voice assistant that only understands certain accents, an app that is unusable with a screen reader, or a service that needs a fast connection and the latest phone all exclude people.",
   "Inclusive design involves people with a wide range of abilities, languages and backgrounds in design and testing, follows accessibility standards, and uses AI to remove barriers, for example captions for people who are deaf or hard of hearing, image descriptions for people who are blind, or translation for people who speak other languages.",
   "The exam often places inclusiveness next to fairness as distractors. Ask yourself: is the problem that a group gets worse outcomes from decisions (fairness), or that a group cannot use the system properly at all (inclusiveness)? And if the scenario is about protecting data or controlling access, it is privacy and security."
  ],
  terms: [["Privacy and security", "The principle that AI systems should protect personal data and be secure against misuse and attack."], ["Inclusiveness", "The principle that AI should empower everyone and be designed to be usable by people of all abilities and backgrounds."], ["Personally identifiable information (PII)", "Data that can identify a person, such as a name, phone number, address or ID number."], ["Data minimization", "Collecting and keeping only the personal data that a purpose actually needs."]],
  example: "A city builds a chatbot for council services. It masks residents' personal details in chat logs, stores them encrypted and limits access to two analysts (privacy and security). It also supports twelve languages, works with screen readers and offers a voice option for people who find typing difficult (inclusiveness).",
  tip: "Data protection, consent, encryption and access control map to privacy and security. Accessibility, languages and designing for people with disabilities map to inclusiveness.",
  check: [["An app adds automatic captions so deaf users can follow video lessons. Which principle is this?", "Inclusiveness, because it makes the system usable by people with different abilities."], ["Before training, a team removes patients' names and ID numbers from the dataset. Which principle does this support?", "Privacy and security, because it protects personal data."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Responsible AI principles: transparency and accountability",
  body: [
   "The last two principles are about understanding and responsibility. Even a fair, safe and secure AI system can lose people's trust if nobody can explain what it does or who is answerable when it goes wrong. Transparency and accountability address those gaps.",
   "Transparency means AI systems should be understandable. People affected by an AI system should know that AI is involved, what it is intended to do, how well it works and what its limitations are. Developers and operators should understand which factors influence a model's output, so they can spot errors and explain decisions.",
   "Transparency shows up in several ways. Users are told when they are talking to an AI or reading AI-generated content. Documentation explains intended uses, data sources and limitations. Explainability tools show which features most influenced a prediction, for example that income and existing debt drove a credit decision. Microsoft publishes transparency notes for its AI services, and Azure Machine Learning's Responsible AI dashboard includes model interpretability features.",
   "Accountability means people should be accountable for AI systems. An AI system is not responsible for its own decisions; the people and organizations that design, deploy and operate it are. Accountability means clear ownership, governance processes, compliance with laws and internal standards, and a way for people to challenge decisions and get them reviewed by a human.",
   "In practice, accountability looks like an AI review board or responsible AI committee that approves high-risk systems, documented policies for when AI can and cannot be used, audit trails of decisions, defined owners for each model, and escalation paths when harm occurs. Microsoft itself follows an internal Responsible AI Standard and restricts some capabilities through its Limited Access policy.",
   "To tell them apart on the exam: if the scenario is about explaining, disclosing, documenting or understanding how the system works, the answer is transparency. If it is about who is responsible, oversight, governance, review processes or answering for outcomes, the answer is accountability."
  ],
  terms: [["Transparency", "The principle that people should understand how an AI system works, what it is for and what its limitations are."], ["Accountability", "The principle that the people who design and deploy AI systems are answerable for how they operate."], ["Explainability", "Showing which inputs or features most influenced a model's output."], ["Transparency note", "Microsoft documentation describing an AI service's capabilities, intended uses and limitations."]],
  example: "A recruitment platform shows candidates that an AI screened their CV and which skills it matched (transparency). The company's AI governance board approved the model, a named product owner reviews monthly reports, and candidates can request a human review of any rejection (accountability).",
  tip: "Explain or disclose points to transparency. Own, govern, review or answer for points to accountability. Both are often paired with Limited Access and transparency notes in questions.",
  check: [["A chatbot tells users at the start of each conversation that they are talking to an AI system. Which principle does this support?", "Transparency, because it discloses that AI is involved."], ["A company requires a human manager to sign off every AI-recommended dismissal and keeps records of each decision. Which principle is this?", "Accountability, because people remain answerable for the system's decisions."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Responsible AI in practice: identifying harms, human oversight, Limited Access features and transparency notes",
  body: [
   "Principles only help if they change how systems are built and run. This lesson covers the practical tools and habits that put responsible AI into action, including some specific Microsoft mechanisms that appear on AI-900.",
   "It starts with identifying potential harms early. Before building, teams ask who could be affected by the system and how: could it deny someone a service unfairly, expose personal data, give dangerous advice, or be misused for surveillance or deception? An impact assessment documents intended uses, stakeholders, possible harms and planned mitigations. Microsoft's Responsible AI Standard asks its own teams to complete one for AI systems.",
   "Human oversight keeps a person involved where the stakes are high. Human-in-the-loop designs have a person approve AI recommendations before they take effect, such as a doctor confirming an AI-suggested diagnosis. Other designs let a human monitor the system and intervene, or let affected people appeal to a human reviewer. Showing confidence scores and routing low-confidence results to people are common patterns.",
   "Limited Access is Microsoft's policy of restricting certain sensitive AI capabilities to approved customers and use cases. Customers must apply, describe their intended use and agree to terms before using features such as face identification and verification in Azure AI Face, or custom neural voice in Azure AI Speech, which can create synthetic voices resembling a real person. The aim is to reduce risks such as unlawful surveillance and impersonation. Microsoft also retired Face capabilities that inferred emotional states and identity attributes such as gender and age, because the science was unreliable and the potential for misuse was high.",
   "Transparency notes and related documentation explain what each Azure AI service can and cannot do, how it was evaluated, and which uses are and are not recommended. Reading them is part of choosing a service responsibly. For generative AI, Microsoft also provides built-in content filtering, Azure AI Content Safety and evaluation tools, covered in domain 5.",
   "Finally, responsible AI continues after launch: monitor performance and fairness over time, collect user feedback, log incidents, and be ready to roll back or switch off a system that causes harm."
  ],
  terms: [["Impact assessment", "A documented review of an AI system's intended uses, stakeholders, potential harms and mitigations."], ["Human in the loop", "A design where a person reviews or approves AI outputs before they take effect."], ["Limited Access", "Microsoft's policy that requires approval of the customer and use case before sensitive AI features can be used."], ["Custom neural voice", "A Speech capability that creates a synthetic voice resembling a specific person; it is a Limited Access feature."]],
  example: "A hospital pilots an AI tool that prioritizes radiology scans. It completes an impact assessment, reads the vendor's transparency documentation, has radiologists confirm every urgent flag before action (human in the loop), and reviews missed cases monthly with a clinical safety officer.",
  tip: "If a question asks why face identification requires an application, the answer is Limited Access for responsible use, not pricing or preview status. Emotion, gender and age inference are no longer offered by Azure AI Face.",
  check: [["Name two Azure AI capabilities that fall under Limited Access.", "Face identification and verification in Azure AI Face, and custom neural voice in Azure AI Speech."], ["What is the purpose of routing low-confidence predictions to a person?", "Human oversight: people review uncertain cases so errors are caught before they cause harm."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Features and labels, training and validation data, and how a model is trained and then used for inference",
  body: [
   "Machine learning starts with data, usually arranged as a table. Each row is an observation (one house, one patient, one transaction) and each column is a value describing it. Understanding what the columns are for is the foundation of every other ML topic on AI-900.",
   "Features are the input columns the model uses to make a prediction. For a house-price model, features might be floor area, number of bedrooms, age and distance to the station. The label is the column you want to predict, here the sale price. Mathematically, a model is a function y = f(x), where x is the set of feature values and y is the predicted label. Training is the process of finding a function that maps x to y well.",
   "When the training data includes known labels, the approach is called supervised learning: the algorithm is supervised by the correct answers. Regression and classification are supervised. When the data has no labels, as in clustering, it is unsupervised learning: the algorithm finds structure on its own.",
   "You never evaluate a model only on the data it learned from, because a model can memorize its training examples and still fail on new ones. Instead you split the data. Most rows (often around 70 to 80 percent) form the training set that the algorithm learns from. The rest are held back as a validation (or test) set. After training, you use the model to predict labels for the validation rows and compare the predictions with the actual labels to calculate metrics.",
   "Overfitting is when a model fits the training data too closely, including its noise, and performs much worse on validation data. Underfitting is when a model is too simple to capture the pattern and performs poorly on both. The goal is a model that generalizes: it performs well on data it has not seen.",
   "Training is iterative. Data scientists try different algorithms, adjust settings called hyperparameters, engineer better features and retrain until validation metrics are good enough. The chosen model is then deployed, and applications send it new feature values to get predictions. That use of the model is inferencing. At inference time there is no label; predicting it is the whole point."
  ],
  terms: [["Feature", "An input value the model uses to make a prediction, such as age or floor area."], ["Label", "The value a supervised model is trained to predict, such as price or a yes/no outcome."], ["Validation data", "Data held back from training and used to measure how well the model performs on unseen examples."], ["Overfitting", "When a model learns the training data too closely and performs poorly on new data."]],
  example: "A bike-sharing company has two years of daily records: temperature, rainfall, day of week, holiday flag and number of rentals. It trains on 80 percent of the days, checks predictions against the other 20 percent, and then deploys the model so tomorrow's weather forecast produces a rental estimate for staffing.",
  tip: "If the question asks which column is the label, pick the value being predicted. A big gap between training and validation performance means overfitting.",
  check: [["In a dataset for predicting whether an email is spam, what is the label?", "The spam or not spam column, because that is what the model predicts; the email's words and sender are features."], ["Why must validation data be kept separate from training data?", "So you can measure how the model performs on data it has not seen, which reveals overfitting."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Regression: predicting a numeric value, with evaluation metrics MAE, RMSE and R²",
  body: [
   "Regression is supervised machine learning that predicts a numeric value. How much will this house sell for? How many ice creams will we sell tomorrow? How many minutes will this delivery take? If the label is a number on a continuous scale, the task is regression.",
   "A simple example is linear regression, which fits a straight line (or, with several features, a flat surface) through the training data so that the predicted value changes steadily as the features change. Other regression algorithms, such as decision trees and neural networks, can capture more complex relationships. On AI-900 you do not need to know how each algorithm works; you need to recognize regression scenarios and read its metrics.",
   "Regression is evaluated by comparing the predicted values with the actual values in the validation set. The differences are called errors or residuals. Mean absolute error (MAE) is the average of the absolute errors. If MAE is 3 for an ice-cream model, predictions are on average 3 ice creams off. It is easy to interpret because it is in the same units as the label.",
   "Mean squared error (MSE) squares each error before averaging, which punishes large errors more heavily than small ones. Root mean squared error (RMSE) is the square root of MSE, which brings it back to the label's units. If RMSE is much larger than MAE, some predictions are badly wrong. For MAE, MSE and RMSE, smaller is better.",
   "The coefficient of determination, written R² (R-squared), measures how much of the variation in the label the model explains. It is usually between 0 and 1. An R² of 0.9 means the model explains 90 percent of the variance in the actual values; closer to 1 is better. Unlike MAE and RMSE, R² has no units, which makes it handy for comparing models.",
   "Regression also underlies forecasting, where the features include time, such as predicting daily demand for the next month. Automated ML in Azure Machine Learning has regression and time-series forecasting task types that try many algorithms and rank them by a metric you choose, such as normalized RMSE."
  ],
  terms: [["Regression", "Supervised learning that predicts a numeric value."], ["Mean absolute error (MAE)", "The average absolute difference between predicted and actual values, in the label's units."], ["Root mean squared error (RMSE)", "The square root of the average squared error; it penalizes large errors more than MAE."], ["Coefficient of determination (R²)", "The proportion of variance in the label explained by the model; closer to 1 is better."]],
  example: "An estate agent trains a regression model on past sales. On validation data it has an MAE of 12,000 and an RMSE of 30,000, showing that most predictions are close but a few luxury houses are badly off. An R² of 0.86 shows the features explain most of the price variation.",
  tip: "Numeric label means regression. Metrics with 'error' in the name should be low; R² should be close to 1. Precision and recall are never regression metrics.",
  check: [["A model predicts the number of hospital beds needed each day. Regression or classification?", "Regression, because the label is a number."], ["Two models have R² of 0.62 and 0.91. Which explains more of the variation?", "The model with R² of 0.91, because R² closer to 1 means more variance is explained."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Binary and multiclass classification, with accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and the confusion matrix",
  body: [
   "Classification is supervised machine learning that predicts which category (class) an item belongs to. Binary classification has two possible classes: spam or not spam, fraud or legitimate, will churn or will stay. Multiclass classification has three or more: which of five teams should get this ticket, which species is this flower, which of ten digits is handwritten here.",
   "A classifier usually calculates a probability for each class. In binary classification, a threshold (often 0.5) turns that probability into a prediction: 0.83 probability of fraud becomes 'fraud'. Changing the threshold trades off different kinds of mistakes, which is why several metrics exist.",
   "Metrics come from the confusion matrix, a table comparing predicted classes with actual classes. For binary classification it has four cells. True positives (TP) are positive cases predicted positive. True negatives (TN) are negative cases predicted negative. False positives (FP) are negative cases wrongly predicted positive, like a real email sent to spam. False negatives (FN) are positive cases wrongly predicted negative, like a fraud that slipped through.",
   "Accuracy is the share of all predictions that were correct: (TP + TN) divided by the total. It can mislead when classes are imbalanced: if 1 percent of transactions are fraud, a model that always says 'legitimate' is 99 percent accurate and useless. Precision is TP / (TP + FP): of the items predicted positive, how many really were. Recall is TP / (TP + FN): of the actual positives, how many the model found. The F1 score combines precision and recall into one number (their harmonic mean).",
   "Which metric matters depends on the cost of each mistake. When missing a positive is costly (disease screening, fraud), favor recall. When a false alarm is costly (flagging a legitimate customer as a criminal, sending important mail to spam), favor precision. Multiclass models have a larger confusion matrix with one row and column per class, and metrics are calculated per class and averaged.",
   "In Azure Machine Learning, automated ML supports classification and reports these metrics, a confusion matrix chart and other visuals for each model it trains, so you can compare candidates."
  ],
  terms: [["Binary classification", "Predicting one of two classes, such as yes or no."], ["Confusion matrix", "A table of actual versus predicted classes showing true and false positives and negatives."], ["Precision", "Of the items predicted positive, the proportion that were actually positive: TP / (TP + FP)."], ["Recall", "Of the actual positive items, the proportion the model found: TP / (TP + FN)."]],
  example: "A diabetes screening model is tested on 1,000 patients, 100 of whom have diabetes. It flags 120 patients, 90 of them correctly. Recall is 90/100 = 0.9 and precision is 90/120 = 0.75. The clinic accepts some false alarms because missing a diabetic patient is worse.",
  tip: "Two classes is binary, more is multiclass, even if the classes are numbers like ratings 1 to 5. Missing positives is costly: recall. False alarms are costly: precision.",
  check: [["A model predicts which of four subscription plans a customer will choose. Which type of classification is this?", "Multiclass classification, because there are more than two classes."], ["In a spam filter, what is a false positive?", "A legitimate email that the model wrongly marked as spam."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Clustering: grouping unlabeled data, and supervised vs unsupervised learning",
  body: [
   "Clustering is unsupervised machine learning that groups similar items together. Unlike regression and classification, there is no label column and no right answer in the training data. The algorithm looks only at the features and decides which items are alike.",
   "The most common example algorithm is k-means. You choose the number of clusters, k. The algorithm places k center points, assigns each item to the nearest center, moves each center to the average of its items, and repeats until the clusters stop changing. The result is a cluster number for each item. It is up to people to interpret what the clusters mean: 'cluster 2 looks like bargain hunters who shop on weekends'.",
   "Clustering is useful when you want to discover structure you did not know about. Businesses use it for customer segmentation (finding groups of customers with similar buying habits for targeted marketing), grouping documents by topic, organizing products with similar characteristics, and exploring data before building other models. It can also help label data: once you understand the clusters, you might name them and use them as classes to train a classifier.",
   "Evaluation is different from supervised learning because there are no actual labels to compare with. Clustering metrics measure how tight each cluster is and how well separated the clusters are, for example the average distance between items and their cluster center, and the distance between cluster centers.",
   "The key AI-900 distinction is supervised versus unsupervised. Supervised learning trains on data that includes known labels, so the model learns to predict those labels: regression (numbers) and classification (categories). Unsupervised learning trains on data without labels and finds patterns on its own: clustering is the example on the exam.",
   "Watch the wording in scenarios. 'Assign each customer to one of our three loyalty tiers' uses known categories, so it is classification. 'Discover groups of customers with similar behavior' with no predefined groups is clustering."
  ],
  terms: [["Clustering", "Unsupervised learning that groups items with similar feature values."], ["Unsupervised learning", "Machine learning on data without labels, which finds structure on its own."], ["Supervised learning", "Machine learning on data that includes known labels, used to predict those labels."], ["k-means", "A clustering algorithm that groups items around k center points, repeatedly adjusting the centers."]],
  example: "An online bookshop runs k-means on customers' purchase frequency, average spend and favorite genres, with k set to 4. It finds a cluster of high-spending crime fans who buy on release day, and designs a pre-order campaign just for them.",
  tip: "No labels and 'discover groups' means clustering. Known categories to predict means classification, even if the scenario uses the word 'group'.",
  check: [["Is clustering supervised or unsupervised, and why?", "Unsupervised, because the training data has no labels; the algorithm finds groups from the features alone."], ["A school wants to predict whether students will pass or fail using last year's results. Clustering or classification?", "Classification, because past results provide known labels (pass or fail)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Deep learning: neural networks, weights and layers, and why they suit images, speech and language",
  body: [
   "Deep learning is a branch of machine learning that uses artificial neural networks with many layers. It is behind nearly all modern computer vision, speech recognition and language models, including the large language models used in generative AI.",
   "A neural network is loosely inspired by the brain. It is made of layers of simple units (artificial neurons). Each neuron takes numbers from the previous layer, multiplies each by a weight, adds them up with a bias value, and passes the result through an activation function that decides how strongly it fires. The first layer receives the input features, such as pixel values; the last layer produces the output, such as a probability for each class. The layers in between are hidden layers, and a network with many of them is called deep.",
   "Training adjusts the weights. The network makes predictions on training data, a loss function measures how wrong they are, and an optimization method (gradient descent, using a technique called backpropagation) nudges every weight in the direction that reduces the loss. This is repeated over many passes through the data (epochs) until the predictions are good. Large networks have millions or billions of weights, which is why training needs lots of data and powerful hardware such as GPUs.",
   "The great strength of deep learning is that it learns useful features automatically. With classic machine learning, a person often had to design features by hand, for example 'number of words in capital letters'. A deep network fed raw pixels learns for itself that early layers should detect edges, later layers shapes and later still whole objects. That makes it ideal for unstructured data such as images, audio and text, where good features are hard to design by hand.",
   "Different architectures suit different data. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) are designed for images. Transformers, covered in the next lesson, dominate language and are increasingly used for images and audio too. Deep learning models can still do regression and classification; they are simply a more powerful kind of model.",
   "For AI-900 you do not need the mathematics. Remember that neural networks are layers of weighted connections, training adjusts the weights to reduce error, and deep learning excels when data is complex and plentiful."
  ],
  terms: [["Neural network", "A model made of layers of connected artificial neurons whose weights are learned during training."], ["Weight", "A number on a connection between neurons that is adjusted during training to reduce error."], ["Deep learning", "Machine learning with neural networks that have many hidden layers."], ["Loss function", "A calculation that measures how far a model's predictions are from the correct answers."]],
  example: "A postal service trains a deep neural network on millions of images of handwritten postcodes. Nobody writes rules for loops and strokes; the early layers learn edges, later layers learn digit shapes, and the output layer gives a probability for each digit.",
  tip: "Deep learning is a subset of machine learning, which is a subset of AI. If a scenario involves images, audio or natural language at scale, deep learning is the technique underneath.",
  check: [["What is adjusted when a neural network is trained?", "The weights (and biases) on the connections between neurons, to reduce the loss."], ["Why is deep learning well suited to images?", "It learns useful features such as edges and shapes directly from raw pixels instead of needing hand-designed features."]]
 },
 {
  t: "The transformer architecture: tokens, embeddings and attention",
  body: [
   "The transformer is the neural network architecture behind today's language models, including the GPT family used in Azure OpenAI. AI-900 expects you to know its main ideas at a conceptual level: tokens, embeddings and attention.",
   "First, text is split into tokens. A token may be a whole word, part of a word or a punctuation mark; a common word might be one token while a rare word is split into several. Each token is mapped to an ID number. Models have a limit on how many tokens they can handle in one request (the context window), and generative AI services usually bill by the number of tokens processed.",
   "Next, each token is represented as an embedding: a vector (a list of numbers) with many dimensions. Embeddings are learned so that tokens with related meanings have vectors that point in similar directions. The vectors for 'dog' and 'puppy' end up close together, while 'dog' and 'invoice' are far apart. Position information is added so the model knows the order of the tokens.",
   "The key innovation is attention. In each layer, attention lets every token look at every other token in the input and weigh how relevant each one is to its own meaning. In 'the bank raised interest rates' versus 'we sat on the river bank', attention to the surrounding words lets the model represent 'bank' differently. Multi-head attention does this several ways at once, capturing different kinds of relationships. Because attention processes all tokens in parallel, transformers train efficiently on huge datasets.",
   "The original transformer had an encoder, which builds rich representations of input text, and a decoder, which generates output text. Encoder-style models such as BERT are good at understanding tasks like classification. Decoder-style models such as GPT generate text by predicting the next token, again and again, based on all the tokens so far.",
   "Training these models on massive amounts of text teaches them grammar, facts and patterns of reasoning. That is why a single pretrained model can be adapted by prompting to summarize, translate, answer questions and write code."
  ],
  terms: [["Token", "A unit of text, such as a word or part of a word, that a language model processes."], ["Embedding", "A vector of numbers representing the meaning of a token or text, where similar meanings are close together."], ["Attention", "A mechanism that lets each token weigh the relevance of the other tokens in the sequence."], ["Transformer", "A neural network architecture built on attention, used by modern language models."]],
  example: "Given the prompt 'The trophy did not fit in the suitcase because it was too big', a transformer uses attention to link 'it' to 'trophy' rather than 'suitcase', and continues the text accordingly.",
  tip: "Tokens are the units of text; embeddings are the vectors that represent meaning; attention relates tokens to each other in context. GPT-style models generate by predicting the next token.",
  check: [["What problem does attention solve?", "It lets the model interpret each word in the context of the other words, so the same word can take different meanings."], ["Why are embeddings useful for search?", "Texts with similar meanings have similar vectors, so you can find related content even when the words differ."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Azure Machine Learning: workspace, studio, data assets, compute instances and compute clusters",
  body: [
   "Azure Machine Learning is Microsoft's cloud platform for data scientists and ML engineers to train, deploy and manage their own machine learning models. Where Azure AI services give you pretrained models through an API, Azure Machine Learning is for when you need a model trained on your own data for your own prediction task.",
   "Everything starts with a workspace, the top-level Azure resource that holds all your machine learning assets: data, compute, experiments, models and endpoints. When you create a workspace, Azure also creates or links supporting resources, including a storage account (for data and files), a key vault (for secrets), Application Insights (for monitoring) and optionally a container registry (for model images).",
   "Azure Machine Learning studio is the web portal for working in the workspace. From it you can upload and browse data, run notebooks, start automated ML jobs, build pipelines in the designer, view job results and metrics, register models and deploy endpoints. Developers can do the same with the Python SDK or the Azure CLI machine learning extension.",
   "Data assets are references to data you use for training, such as a file or folder in Azure Blob Storage or a table. Registering data as an asset gives it a name and version, so experiments are reproducible and teammates reuse the same data. Datastores hold the connection information to the underlying storage.",
   "Compute is where the work runs, and it is where the cost is. A compute instance is a managed cloud virtual machine for one data scientist, used for notebooks and development; you should stop it when you are not using it. A compute cluster is a set of VMs that scales out automatically to run training jobs and can scale down to zero nodes when idle, so you only pay while jobs run. Models can also be deployed to managed online endpoints, and you can attach existing compute such as a Kubernetes cluster.",
   "Azure Machine Learning also tracks each training job, its parameters, metrics and outputs, and keeps a model registry with versions. That history supports accountability and makes it possible to reproduce or roll back a model later."
  ],
  terms: [["Workspace", "The top-level Azure Machine Learning resource that holds data, compute, jobs, models and endpoints."], ["Azure Machine Learning studio", "The web portal for working with an Azure Machine Learning workspace."], ["Compute instance", "A managed development VM for one user's notebooks and experiments."], ["Compute cluster", "A group of VMs that scales automatically for training jobs and can scale to zero when idle."]],
  example: "A retail analytics team creates one workspace per project. Each analyst has a small compute instance that auto-stops in the evening, training jobs run on a compute cluster that scales from zero to eight nodes, and the sales history is registered as a versioned data asset so every experiment uses the same data.",
  tip: "Compute instance is for development by one person; compute cluster is for scalable training jobs. Azure Machine Learning is for training your own models; Azure AI services give you prebuilt ones.",
  check: [["Which Azure Machine Learning compute type can scale to zero nodes to save cost between training jobs?", "A compute cluster."], ["When would you choose Azure Machine Learning over an Azure AI service?", "When you need to train a custom model on your own data for a prediction task that prebuilt services do not cover, such as predicting your own sales."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Automated machine learning (AutoML) and the Azure Machine Learning designer",
  body: [
   "Building a good model usually means trying many algorithms, data preparation steps and settings, then comparing the results. Azure Machine Learning offers two low-code ways to do this without writing training code yourself: automated machine learning and the designer. AI-900 asks you to recognize what each one is for.",
   "Automated machine learning (AutoML) automates the search for the best model. You choose a registered dataset, pick the task type (classification, regression, time-series forecasting, and also some computer vision and NLP tasks), name the label column, choose the primary metric (for example accuracy or normalized RMSE) and set limits such as the maximum training time. AutoML then tries many combinations of algorithms and preprocessing, trains each on compute, and ranks the resulting models by the metric.",
   "AutoML also does featurization automatically: filling in missing values, encoding text categories as numbers and scaling values. It can apply guardrails, such as detecting class imbalance, and it shows explanations of which features mattered most. When the job finishes, you review the leaderboard, open the best model's metrics and charts, and deploy it with a few clicks.",
   "The designer is a drag-and-drop canvas for building machine learning pipelines visually. You drag components onto the canvas, such as a dataset, a data cleaning step, a split of the data into training and test sets, a training algorithm, a scoring step and an evaluation step, and connect them. Submitting the pipeline runs each step on compute. You choose the algorithm and steps yourself, so the designer suits people who understand ML concepts but prefer not to code.",
   "In short, AutoML answers 'which algorithm and settings work best for this data?' by trying them for you. The designer answers 'let me build my own workflow visually'. Both run in Azure Machine Learning studio, use workspace compute and produce models you can register and deploy.",
   "A third option is code: data scientists can write Python in notebooks with frameworks such as scikit-learn or PyTorch and submit jobs through the SDK. The exam focuses on knowing that the low-code tools exist and when to choose them."
  ],
  terms: [["Automated machine learning (AutoML)", "A feature that tries many algorithms and settings automatically and ranks the resulting models by a chosen metric."], ["Primary metric", "The measure AutoML uses to rank models, such as accuracy or normalized RMSE."], ["Designer", "A drag-and-drop canvas in Azure Machine Learning studio for building training pipelines visually."], ["Featurization", "Preparing raw data for training, such as handling missing values and encoding categories."]],
  example: "A small insurance company has no data scientists. An analyst uploads five years of claim data, starts an AutoML classification job to predict whether a claim will be disputed, sets AUC as the primary metric and a one-hour limit, then deploys the top model from the leaderboard.",
  tip: "Try many algorithms automatically and pick the best: AutoML. Build a pipeline visually by connecting components: designer. Neither requires writing code.",
  check: [["What must you specify when you start an AutoML job?", "The data, the task type (such as classification or regression), the label column, the primary metric and compute and time limits."], ["How does the designer differ from AutoML?", "In the designer you choose and connect the steps and algorithm yourself on a canvas; AutoML chooses and compares algorithms for you."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Deploying models to endpoints for real-time or batch inference, and responsible AI tools in Azure Machine Learning",
  body: [
   "A model only creates value when applications can use it. In Azure Machine Learning, you deploy a trained model to an endpoint, a web address that accepts input data and returns predictions. AI-900 expects you to know the two main endpoint types and the responsible AI tools that help you check a model before and after deployment.",
   "First you register the model in the workspace's model registry, giving it a name and version. Models trained with AutoML or the designer can be registered and deployed directly from studio. A deployment packages the model with the code and environment needed to run it, on compute that Azure manages for you.",
   "A real-time endpoint (called an online endpoint) serves predictions on demand with low latency. An app sends a request, typically JSON over HTTPS with the feature values for one or a few items, and gets a prediction back within moments. Use it when a person or process is waiting for an answer: approving a loan application, recommending a product on a web page, checking a transaction as it happens. Online endpoints can have several deployments behind them so you can test a new model version with a share of traffic.",
   "A batch endpoint scores large amounts of data asynchronously. You point it at a folder or dataset, it runs a job on a compute cluster, and it writes the predictions to storage. Use it when nobody is waiting, such as scoring every customer overnight for churn risk. Batch scoring is usually cheaper for large volumes because the compute runs only during the job.",
   "Clients authenticate to endpoints with a key or a Microsoft Entra ID token, and you should monitor deployed models for errors, latency and drift in the data they receive.",
   "The Responsible AI dashboard in Azure Machine Learning brings several tools together for a trained model: error analysis (which groups of data the model gets wrong most), fairness assessment (how performance differs across sensitive groups), model interpretability (which features drive predictions, overall and for individual cases), counterfactual what-if analysis (what would need to change for a different outcome) and data analysis. A scorecard can summarize the findings for stakeholders who are not data scientists. These tools put the fairness, reliability and transparency principles into practice."
  ],
  terms: [["Endpoint", "A web address where a deployed model accepts input data and returns predictions."], ["Online (real-time) endpoint", "An endpoint that returns predictions immediately for individual requests."], ["Batch endpoint", "An endpoint that scores large volumes of data asynchronously as a job and writes results to storage."], ["Responsible AI dashboard", "An Azure Machine Learning tool combining error analysis, fairness, interpretability and what-if analysis for a model."]],
  example: "An energy supplier deploys its churn model twice. The call centre app uses an online endpoint to show a live churn-risk score while an agent talks to a customer. Every Sunday, a batch endpoint scores all 2 million customers so marketing can plan the week's retention offers. Before launch, the Responsible AI dashboard showed the model was less accurate for rural customers, so the team added more rural data.",
  tip: "Someone waiting for an immediate answer means a real-time (online) endpoint. Scoring a large dataset on a schedule means batch. Questions about finding where a model makes more errors or treats groups differently point to the Responsible AI dashboard.",
  check: [["A web shop needs product recommendations while the customer is browsing. Which endpoint type fits?", "A real-time (online) endpoint, because the answer is needed immediately for each request."], ["Name three tools in the Responsible AI dashboard.", "Error analysis, fairness assessment and model interpretability; counterfactual what-if analysis and data analysis are also included."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Image classification vs object detection vs semantic segmentation",
  body: [
   "Many computer vision questions on AI-900 come down to one decision: how much detail about the image does the scenario need? Is one answer for the whole image enough, does it need the location of each object, or does it need the exact shape of each object? Those three levels are image classification, object detection and semantic segmentation.",
   "Image classification predicts a label for the whole image. The model looks at all the pixels and returns the class it thinks the image belongs to, usually with a confidence score, for example 'apple: 0.97'. Some classifiers are multilabel and return several tags for one image. Classification answers 'what is this a picture of?' but not 'where?' or 'how many?'. Typical uses are sorting product photos into categories, identifying plant species or flagging whether an X-ray looks normal.",
   "Object detection finds each instance of the objects it knows about and returns, for every one, a class label, a confidence score and a bounding box: the coordinates of a rectangle around it. It answers 'what objects are in this picture, where are they and how many?'. Typical uses are counting vehicles in traffic footage, checking whether workers wear helmets, spotting empty shelf spaces or locating defects on a production line.",
   "Semantic segmentation classifies each pixel in the image, so the output is a mask showing exactly which pixels belong to which class, such as road, pavement, car and sky. It gives precise shapes instead of rectangles, which matters in medical imaging (the exact outline of a tumour), self-driving cars (exactly where the road ends) and satellite imagery (the area of flooded land). Instance segmentation is a related technique that also separates individual objects of the same class.",
   "These tasks are progressively more detailed and generally need more detailed training labels: a single tag per image for classification, boxes drawn around objects for detection and pixel-level masks for segmentation. Choose the simplest one that meets the requirement.",
   "Image analysis in Azure AI Vision provides prebuilt tagging and object detection for thousands of everyday things. When you need your own classes, such as your company's product line or specific defects, you train a custom model from your labeled images."
  ],
  terms: [["Image classification", "Predicting one or more labels for an image as a whole."], ["Object detection", "Locating each object in an image with a class label and bounding box."], ["Semantic segmentation", "Classifying every pixel in an image to produce a precise mask of each class."], ["Confidence score", "A value between 0 and 1 showing how sure the model is about a prediction."]],
  example: "A farm uses drones over its fields. Image classification labels each photo as 'healthy crop' or 'needs inspection'; object detection counts and locates individual sheep; semantic segmentation measures exactly how much of the field is waterlogged.",
  tip: "Whole image, one answer: classification. Where and how many: object detection with bounding boxes. Exact outline, pixel by pixel: segmentation.",
  check: [["A parking app must show which of 50 spaces in a camera image are occupied. Which technique fits?", "Object detection, because it locates each car with a bounding box, so occupied spaces can be identified and counted."], ["What does semantic segmentation provide that object detection does not?", "A pixel-level mask with the exact shape of each region, rather than a rectangle."]]
 },
 {
  t: "How computer vision models work: pixels, filters, convolutional neural networks and multimodal models",
  body: [
   "To a computer, a digital image is an array of numbers. A grayscale image is a grid of pixel values, typically from 0 (black) to 255 (white). A color image has three such grids, one each for red, green and blue channels. Every computer vision technique starts from these numbers.",
   "A classic image-processing idea is the filter (also called a kernel): a small grid of weights, such as 3 by 3, that slides across the image. At each position, the filter's weights are multiplied by the pixel values beneath it and summed to produce a new value. This operation is called convolution. Depending on the weights, a filter can blur the image, sharpen it or highlight edges. The output of applying a filter is called a feature map.",
   "A convolutional neural network (CNN) is a deep learning model that learns its own filters. Instead of a person choosing weights to detect edges, training adjusts the filter values so that they pick out whatever helps the network classify images correctly. Early layers tend to learn simple features like edges and corners; deeper layers combine them into textures, shapes and eventually whole object parts, such as wheels or eyes. Pooling layers shrink the feature maps to keep the most important information, and the final fully connected layers turn the features into class probabilities.",
   "Training a CNN needs many labeled images. The network predicts a label for each training image, the loss function measures the error, and the filter weights and other weights are adjusted over many epochs. Pretrained networks learned on millions of images can be adapted to new classes with far fewer images, a technique called transfer learning.",
   "More recent vision models use the transformer architecture as well. Multimodal models are trained on huge numbers of images paired with text captions, so they learn a shared representation of images and language. That lets a single model describe images in sentences, find images that match a text query, or answer questions about an image. Captioning in Azure AI Vision and the image input capability of generative models such as GPT-4o are built on this kind of multimodal learning.",
   "For AI-900, remember the chain: pixels are numbers, filters extract features, CNNs learn filters to classify images, and multimodal models connect images with language."
  ],
  terms: [["Pixel", "The smallest element of a digital image, stored as one or more numeric values."], ["Filter (kernel)", "A small grid of weights applied across an image to produce a feature map, for example to highlight edges."], ["Convolutional neural network (CNN)", "A deep learning model that learns filters to extract features from images for tasks such as classification."], ["Multimodal model", "A model trained on more than one type of data, such as images and text together."]],
  example: "A manufacturer adapts a pretrained CNN to spot cracks in ceramic tiles using only a few hundred labeled photos (transfer learning). Later it tries a multimodal model that can answer free-text questions such as 'Is there a chip on the left edge?' about any photo.",
  tip: "CNNs learn their filters during training rather than using hand-chosen ones. A model that can answer questions about images or match images to text is multimodal.",
  check: [["What does a filter produce when applied across an image?", "A feature map that highlights certain patterns, such as edges."], ["Why can a multimodal model generate an image caption?", "It is trained on images paired with text, so it learns to relate visual features to language."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Azure AI Vision image analysis: captions, dense captions, tags, object detection, people detection and smart crops",
  body: [
   "Azure AI Vision is the Azure AI service for analyzing images and video. Its image analysis feature gives you pretrained vision capabilities through a single API call: you send an image (as a URL or the image bytes) and say which visual features you want back. No training is needed. AI-900 expects you to match each feature to a scenario.",
   "Captions generate a human-readable sentence describing the whole image, such as 'a man riding a bicycle down a city street', with a confidence score. Dense captions generate captions for up to several regions of the image, each with a bounding box, so you get a description of different parts of the scene. Captions are ideal for accessibility alt text and for making photo libraries searchable.",
   "Tags return a list of words for things, settings and actions visible in the image, such as 'outdoor', 'tree', 'dog', 'grass', each with a confidence score. Tags are useful for indexing and search. Object detection returns objects with their bounding boxes and labels, for example three 'car' objects with coordinates. People detection specifically finds people and returns their bounding boxes, which supports counting and occupancy scenarios without identifying anyone.",
   "Smart crops suggest a crop region that keeps the most important part of the image for a given aspect ratio, useful for generating thumbnails. The Read (OCR) feature extracts text, covered in its own lesson. Depending on the API version, there are also features such as background removal and multimodal embeddings for image search; check the current documentation for what your version supports.",
   "To use the service, you create an Azure AI Vision resource (or a multi-service Azure AI services resource) and call its endpoint with a key or Microsoft Entra ID. You can explore the features without code in the vision try-it experience in the Microsoft Foundry portal or in Vision Studio, and then use the REST API or SDKs from your app. Some features are only available in certain regions.",
   "When a scenario needs categories specific to your business, such as your own product models, prebuilt image analysis may not know them; that is when you train a custom model with your own labeled images."
  ],
  terms: [["Caption", "A generated sentence describing an image, returned with a confidence score."], ["Dense captions", "Captions for multiple regions of an image, each with a bounding box."], ["Tag", "A word describing something visible in an image, such as an object, setting or action."], ["Smart crop", "A suggested crop region that keeps the area of interest for a given aspect ratio."]],
  example: "A museum uploads 40,000 digitized photos. Image analysis adds captions for screen-reader users, tags such as 'ship', 'harbour' and 'black and white' for search, smart crops for gallery thumbnails, and people detection to find photos that include visitors.",
  tip: "Sentence describing the image: captions. List of keywords: tags. Where things are: object or people detection. Thumbnail region: smart crops. Text in the image: OCR (Read).",
  check: [["Which image analysis feature best produces alt text for a screen reader?", "Captions, because they generate a readable sentence describing the image."], ["How do dense captions differ from captions?", "Dense captions describe several regions of the image, each with its own bounding box, instead of one sentence for the whole image."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Optical character recognition (OCR) with the Azure AI Vision Read feature",
  body: [
   "Optical character recognition (OCR) is the computer vision task of detecting and extracting text from images. It turns pixels that look like letters into machine-readable text that can be searched, stored, translated or analyzed. In Azure, the main OCR capability is the Read feature, available through Azure AI Vision and also used inside Azure AI Document Intelligence.",
   "The Read feature handles printed text in many languages and handwritten text in a number of languages. It works on photos of the real world (signs, labels, whiteboards, number plates, product packaging) and on scanned documents and PDFs. It uses deep learning models, so it copes with different fonts, angles, backgrounds and mixed text styles far better than older template-based OCR.",
   "The results are structured by position. For images, Read returns blocks of text broken into lines and words. Each line and word comes with a bounding polygon (the coordinates of its outline in the image) and each word with a confidence score. For multi-page documents, results are organized by page. That positional data lets an app highlight where text appears, keep reading order or crop out a specific region.",
   "In image analysis you request the Read feature alongside other features in a single call, which suits short text in photos. For longer or multi-page documents, and whenever you need structure such as tables, key-value pairs and fields, Azure AI Document Intelligence builds on the same OCR engine and adds layout and field extraction.",
   "OCR is often the first step in a larger solution. Extracted text can be passed to Azure AI Language for entity recognition or sentiment, to Azure AI Translator to translate signage, or into a search index for knowledge mining. It also supports accessibility by reading printed text aloud with text to speech.",
   "Remember that OCR returns text, not meaning. It will tell you that the characters '1,250.00' appear at a certain position on a receipt, but not that they are the total. For named fields, use Document Intelligence."
  ],
  terms: [["Optical character recognition (OCR)", "Extracting printed or handwritten text from images and documents."], ["Read feature", "The Azure AI Vision OCR capability that returns lines and words with their positions and confidence."], ["Bounding polygon", "The set of coordinates outlining where a line or word appears in the image."], ["Handwriting recognition", "OCR of handwritten rather than printed text."]],
  example: "A delivery firm's drivers photograph handwritten notes left on doors. The app runs Read to extract the text, sends it to translation if needed, and stores it with the delivery record so customer service can search it later.",
  tip: "Extract text from images: OCR with Read. Extract named fields or tables from forms: Document Intelligence. Describe the image in words: captions, not OCR.",
  check: [["What does the Read feature return for each word?", "The text, its position as a bounding polygon and a confidence score."], ["Can Read extract handwriting?", "Yes, it supports handwritten text as well as printed text, in a subset of languages."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Face detection and analysis with Azure AI Face, and the Limited Access policy for identification and verification",
  body: [
   "Azure AI Face is the Azure AI service for detecting and analyzing human faces in images. It is a good example of how capability and responsibility go together in AI-900: you need to know what the service does and which parts are restricted.",
   "Face detection finds faces in an image and returns a bounding box for each one. It can also return landmarks (points such as the corners of the eyes and the tip of the nose) and a set of attributes that describe the image of the face rather than the person: head pose (how the head is turned), whether the person is wearing glasses or a mask, whether the face is blurred, how well it is exposed, whether parts are occluded, and quality for recognition. Detection is useful for tasks such as counting faces, cropping profile photos, blurring faces for privacy or checking that a photo meets ID photo requirements.",
   "Face recognition compares faces. Verification checks whether two faces belong to the same person (one-to-one), for example matching a selfie to an ID photo. Identification finds who a face belongs to by searching a group of known people (one-to-many), for example matching a person at a door to a list of employees. Recognition needs a face template stored for each known person.",
   "Because recognition can be misused for surveillance or tracking, Microsoft makes face identification and verification Limited Access features. A customer must apply, describe the use case, be approved and agree to terms before using them. Some detection attributes are restricted in a similar way.",
   "Microsoft also retired Face capabilities that inferred emotional states (such as happy or angry) and identity attributes such as gender and age, along with others like smile and facial hair. These were removed because the scientific basis for inferring emotion from facial expressions is weak and the attributes could enable discrimination. If a question proposes using Azure AI Face to detect customers' emotions, that is no longer available.",
   "Facial analysis should always consider consent, privacy law, fairness across demographic groups and transparency to the people being analyzed. When a scenario only needs to count or locate people without faces, people detection in Azure AI Vision may be a less sensitive choice."
  ],
  terms: [["Face detection", "Finding faces in an image and returning their location and image attributes."], ["Face verification", "Checking whether two face images belong to the same person (one-to-one)."], ["Face identification", "Finding which known person a face belongs to from a group (one-to-many)."], ["Facial landmarks", "Points on a face, such as eye corners and nose tip, returned by face detection."]],
  example: "A photo-ID kiosk uses face detection to check that each passport photo has one face, facing forward, not blurred and without glasses. The same company's plan to verify travellers against their passport photos requires an approved Limited Access application first.",
  tip: "Detection and image attributes such as blur, glasses and head pose: available. Identification and verification: Limited Access. Emotion, gender and age: retired.",
  check: [["What is the difference between verification and identification?", "Verification compares two faces to confirm they are the same person (one-to-one); identification searches a group to find who someone is (one-to-many)."], ["Why did Microsoft retire emotion inference from Azure AI Face?", "Because inferring emotions from facial expressions lacks a reliable scientific basis and carries a high risk of misuse and unfair outcomes."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Azure AI Document Intelligence: prebuilt models (invoices, receipts, IDs), the layout model and custom models",
  body: [
   "Azure AI Document Intelligence (formerly Form Recognizer) is the Azure AI service for extracting text, structure and data from documents. It builds on OCR but returns much more: the layout of the page and, with the right model, named fields with values, ready to store in a database or pass to a business system.",
   "Prebuilt models are trained by Microsoft on common document types and need no training from you. Examples include invoices (vendor, customer, invoice number, dates, line items, totals), receipts (merchant, date, items, subtotal, tax, total), identity documents such as passports and driving licences, business cards, and several tax and financial forms. Each extracted field comes with its value, its location on the page and a confidence score, so an app can route low-confidence documents to a person.",
   "The layout model extracts text, tables (with rows, columns and cells), selection marks such as checkboxes, paragraphs and document structure from any document, without looking for specific named fields. The read model extracts text only. A general document option also finds key-value pairs. Use layout when you need structure from documents that no prebuilt model covers, or as a first step before custom processing.",
   "Custom models handle document types unique to your organization. A custom extraction model learns to extract the fields you define from a set of your sample documents that you label in Document Intelligence Studio; a handful of labeled examples can be enough for a consistent form layout. A custom classification model identifies which type of document a file is, so you can route it to the right extraction model.",
   "You create a Document Intelligence resource (or use a multi-service Azure AI services resource), then try models in Document Intelligence Studio without code, or call them through the REST API and SDKs. Documents can be sent as images or PDFs. A free F0 tier with limited pages is available for experiments.",
   "The main exam distinction is between plain OCR and document intelligence. OCR in Azure AI Vision gives text and positions. Document Intelligence gives you meaning and structure: which text is the total, which rows form a table, which box is ticked."
  ],
  terms: [["Prebuilt model", "A Document Intelligence model trained by Microsoft for a common document type, such as invoices or receipts."], ["Layout model", "A model that extracts text, tables, selection marks and structure from any document."], ["Custom extraction model", "A model you train with labeled samples to extract your own fields from your own document type."], ["Selection mark", "A checkbox or radio button on a form, returned as selected or unselected."]],
  example: "An expenses app lets staff photograph receipts. The prebuilt receipt model returns the merchant, date, tax and total, and the app fills in the expense claim automatically. The company's own mileage form has a unique layout, so the team labels ten examples and trains a custom extraction model for it.",
  tip: "Common business document with standard fields: prebuilt model. Tables and structure from any document: layout. Your own unique form: custom model trained on labeled samples.",
  check: [["A company needs line items and totals from supplier invoices. Which model should it try first?", "The prebuilt invoice model, because it already extracts those fields without training."], ["What does a custom classification model do?", "It identifies the type of each document so it can be sent to the right extraction model."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Creating and using Azure AI services resources: multi-service vs single-service resources, endpoints, keys and the free F0 tier",
  body: [
   "Before your app can call any Azure AI service, you need a resource for it in your Azure subscription. The resource gives you an endpoint to call, credentials to authenticate and a place for billing. AI-900 tests the choices you make when creating one.",
   "A multi-service resource, named Azure AI services in the portal, provides access to several AI services, such as Vision, Language, Speech, Translator and Document Intelligence, through one resource with one set of keys and consolidated billing. It suits developers who use several services and want to manage them together. Azure OpenAI models and other generative AI models can also be used through Foundry resources, covered in domain 5.",
   "A single-service resource provides access to just one service, such as Azure AI Vision or Azure AI Language. Choose it when you use only that service, want to track its costs separately, need a separate access boundary, or want the free tier. Most single-service resources offer a free F0 pricing tier with limited transactions per month, which is enough for learning and prototypes; the standard tiers (such as S0) are paid per use. The multi-service resource does not have a free tier.",
   "Every resource has an endpoint, a URL your app sends requests to, and two keys. Clients authenticate by sending a key in a request header, or, preferably in production, by using Microsoft Entra ID so that no key is stored in code. There are two keys so you can regenerate one while apps use the other, then switch, without downtime. Keys should be kept in a secure store such as Azure Key Vault, never in source code.",
   "You also choose a region, which affects latency, data residency and which features are available, and a resource group for management. After creating the resource, you can test it without code in the Foundry portal or the service's studio (such as Vision Studio or Language Studio), then use the REST API or SDKs from your application.",
   "When you finish a lab, delete the resource or its resource group so nothing keeps billing, and prefer F0 where the service offers it."
  ],
  terms: [["Multi-service resource", "An Azure AI services resource that gives one endpoint and set of keys for several AI services with one bill."], ["Single-service resource", "A resource for one AI service, such as Azure AI Vision, often with a free F0 tier."], ["Endpoint", "The URL an application calls to use the AI service."], ["Resource key", "A secret value sent with requests to authenticate to an AI service; each resource has two."]],
  example: "A student building a class demo creates a single-service Azure AI Vision resource on the F0 tier, stores the endpoint and key in environment variables, and tests the caption feature. Her employer's production app instead uses one multi-service Azure AI services resource for Vision, Language and Translator, with Microsoft Entra ID authentication and one consolidated bill.",
  tip: "One key and endpoint for many services, one bill: multi-service. Free F0 tier or separate billing for one service: single-service. An app needs the endpoint plus a key (or Entra ID) to call it.",
  check: [["Why does each Azure AI services resource have two keys?", "So you can rotate keys without downtime: apps switch to the second key while the first is regenerated."], ["Which resource type should you pick to experiment with Azure AI Language at no cost?", "A single-service Language resource on the free F0 tier, where available."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Choosing the right Azure vision service for a scenario",
  body: [
   "Many AI-900 vision questions describe a business need and ask which service or feature to use. The fastest way to answer is to identify the input and the output the scenario wants, then match them to the service. This lesson brings the domain together as a decision guide.",
   "If the input is a photo or video frame and the output is a description of what is in it, use Azure AI Vision image analysis. Choose the specific feature by the output: a sentence (captions), sentences for regions (dense captions), keywords (tags), objects with locations (object detection), people with locations (people detection) or a thumbnail region (smart crops).",
   "If the output is the text in the image, use OCR with the Read feature in Azure AI Vision. If the input is a document such as an invoice, receipt, ID or form and the output is structured data (named fields, tables, key-value pairs, checkboxes), use Azure AI Document Intelligence: a prebuilt model for common documents, the layout model for structure from any document and a custom model for your own forms.",
   "If the scenario is about faces, use Azure AI Face. Detection and image attributes such as blur, glasses or head pose are available. Verification and identification require Limited Access approval. Inferring emotion, gender or age is not offered. If the scenario only needs to count people, people detection in Azure AI Vision avoids face analysis altogether.",
   "If the scenario needs categories that prebuilt models do not know, such as your company's own products, parts or defects, you need a custom vision model trained on your own labeled images, for example with Azure Machine Learning's AutoML image tasks. If the scenario wants to ask free-form questions about an image or combine image understanding with reasoning, a multimodal generative model (such as a GPT model that accepts images, deployed through Microsoft Foundry) is the fit.",
   "Finally, check the non-functional hints: free experimentation suggests a single-service F0 resource; one key for several services suggests a multi-service resource; sensitive face scenarios bring in Limited Access and responsible AI."
  ],
  terms: [["Azure AI Vision", "The Azure AI service for image analysis and OCR with prebuilt models."], ["Azure AI Face", "The Azure AI service for detecting and analyzing faces, with recognition under Limited Access."], ["Azure AI Document Intelligence", "The Azure AI service that extracts fields, tables and structure from documents."], ["Custom vision model", "An image model trained on your own labeled images to recognize classes that prebuilt models do not cover."]],
  example: "A logistics company has four needs: read container codes from dock photos (Vision OCR), extract fields from bills of lading (Document Intelligence), count workers on a loading bay (Vision people detection) and detect damage types specific to its containers (a custom model trained on labeled photos).",
  tip: "Map output to service: description or objects to Vision image analysis, text to Vision Read, fields and tables to Document Intelligence, faces to Face, your own classes to a custom model.",
  check: [["A company wants the merchant name and total from photographed receipts. Which service?", "Azure AI Document Intelligence with the prebuilt receipt model."], ["A museum needs a sentence describing each photo for visually impaired visitors. Which service and feature?", "Azure AI Vision image analysis with captions."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Language detection, sentiment analysis and opinion mining",
  body: [
   "Azure AI Language is the Azure AI service for understanding and analyzing text. It offers a set of prebuilt features that you call through one resource and one API, plus features you can customize. This lesson covers three of the most tested prebuilt features: language detection, sentiment analysis and opinion mining.",
   "Language detection identifies the language a piece of text is written in. It returns the language name, its ISO code (such as 'fr' for French) and a confidence score between 0 and 1. It is useful for routing messages to the right team, choosing the right translation or processing pipeline, and tagging content. Very short or mixed-language text can give lower confidence; if the language cannot be determined, the service returns '(Unknown)' with a score of 0 rather than guessing.",
   "Sentiment analysis evaluates whether text expresses a positive, neutral or negative opinion. It returns a label for the whole document and for each sentence, along with confidence scores for positive, neutral and negative that add up to 1. A document containing both strongly positive and negative sentences can be labelled mixed. Typical uses are analyzing product reviews, social media posts, survey comments and support transcripts to spot unhappy customers or measure reaction to a launch.",
   "Opinion mining is an option of sentiment analysis that goes finer. It links opinions to specific targets (aspects) in the text. For 'The screen is gorgeous but the battery dies too fast', document-level sentiment might just say mixed, while opinion mining reports that 'screen' has a positive opinion ('gorgeous') and 'battery' has a negative one ('dies too fast'). Businesses use it to learn exactly which product features people like or dislike.",
   "You can try these features without code in Language Studio or the language playground in the Microsoft Foundry portal, then call the REST API or SDK from your app, sending documents in batches. Results always include confidence scores, so your app can decide how to treat uncertain results.",
   "Remember what these features do not do: they do not translate text, extract topics or create new text. They analyze existing text and return labels and scores."
  ],
  terms: [["Azure AI Language", "The Azure AI service that analyzes and understands text with prebuilt and customizable features."], ["Language detection", "Identifying the language of text and returning its name, ISO code and a confidence score."], ["Sentiment analysis", "Labeling text or sentences as positive, neutral, negative or mixed with confidence scores."], ["Opinion mining", "Aspect-based sentiment that links opinions to specific targets mentioned in the text."]],
  example: "A phone maker analyzes 50,000 reviews. Language detection sorts them by language, sentiment analysis shows 72 percent are positive overall, and opinion mining reveals that most negative opinions target 'battery' and 'charger', guiding the next design.",
  tip: "Overall mood of text: sentiment analysis. Mood toward specific features mentioned: opinion mining. Which language: language detection. None of them translate.",
  check: [["What three sentiment scores does sentiment analysis return for each sentence?", "Confidence scores for positive, neutral and negative, which add up to 1."], ["A restaurant wants to know how customers feel about its desserts and its service separately. Which feature?", "Opinion mining, because it links sentiment to specific aspects such as desserts and service."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Key phrase extraction, named entity recognition, entity linking and PII detection",
  body: [
   "The next group of Azure AI Language features pulls specific information out of text. They answer questions like 'what is this about?', 'who and what is mentioned?' and 'is there personal data here?'. All are prebuilt, so you call them without training anything.",
   "Key phrase extraction identifies the main talking points in a text and returns them as a list of phrases, such as 'delivery delay', 'refund request' and 'customer service team'. It works best on longer text and helps summarize what documents are about at a glance, tag content for search, or spot trending topics across many messages.",
   "Named entity recognition (NER) finds references to entities and classifies them into categories such as Person, Location, Organization, DateTime, Quantity, Event, Product, URL, Email and PhoneNumber, some with subcategories. For 'Maria flew to Tokyo on 3 May to meet Contoso', NER returns Maria (Person), Tokyo (Location), 3 May (DateTime) and Contoso (Organization), each with its position in the text and a confidence score.",
   "Entity linking goes a step further for well-known entities. It disambiguates which real-world thing an entity refers to, using the context, and links it to an entry in a knowledge base, typically Wikipedia. 'Mars' in an article about chocolate bars and 'Mars' in an article about space missions are linked to different entries. This is useful when the same name can mean different things.",
   "Personally identifiable information (PII) detection finds sensitive personal data such as names, addresses, phone numbers, email addresses, government ID numbers and bank or credit card numbers. It returns the entities found and a redacted version of the text in which they are masked, for example with asterisks. There is also a health-focused feature, Text Analytics for health, that extracts medical entities such as conditions and medications.",
   "These features often work together. A support system might extract key phrases to tag each ticket, run NER to pick out product names and dates, and run PII detection to redact card numbers before transcripts are stored or shared. That last step also supports the privacy and security principle of responsible AI."
  ],
  terms: [["Key phrase extraction", "Returning the main talking points of a text as a list of phrases."], ["Named entity recognition (NER)", "Finding entities in text and classifying them as types such as Person, Location or Organization."], ["Entity linking", "Identifying which known real-world entity a mention refers to and linking it to a knowledge base entry."], ["PII detection", "Finding personal information in text and returning a redacted version."]],
  example: "A newsroom tags each article: key phrases become search tags, NER lists the people and places mentioned, entity linking makes sure 'Jordan' links to the country rather than the basketball player in a foreign affairs story, and PII detection strips readers' phone numbers from published comments.",
  tip: "Main topics: key phrases. Categorized mentions such as people and dates: NER. Disambiguate and link to Wikipedia: entity linking. Find and mask personal data: PII detection.",
  check: [["Which feature returns a redacted version of the text?", "PII detection, which masks the personal information it finds."], ["What does entity linking add beyond NER?", "It disambiguates which real-world entity is meant and links it to a knowledge base entry such as a Wikipedia page."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Tokenization, embeddings and semantic similarity: how text becomes numbers",
  body: [
   "Machine learning models work with numbers, not words. Every NLP system, from a sentiment classifier to a large language model, must first turn text into numeric form. AI-900 expects you to understand the main steps conceptually: tokenization, frequency-based representations and embeddings.",
   "Tokenization splits text into tokens, the units a model processes. Simple approaches split on spaces and punctuation, so 'The cat sat.' becomes 'The', 'cat', 'sat', '.'. Modern language models use subword tokenization, which breaks rare words into common pieces so the vocabulary stays manageable while any word can still be represented. Before or during tokenization, text may be normalized, for example converted to lowercase, and very common stop words such as 'the' or 'a' may be removed for simpler statistical models.",
   "Early NLP represented text by counting. Term frequency counts how often each word appears in a document. Term frequency-inverse document frequency (TF-IDF) weighs words that appear often in one document but rarely across the collection, so distinctive words stand out. N-grams capture short sequences such as 'not good', which carry meaning single words miss. These representations power simple classifiers and key phrase techniques but do not capture meaning well: 'car' and 'automobile' look completely unrelated.",
   "Embeddings solve that. An embedding is a vector (a list of many numbers) learned by a model so that texts with similar meanings are close together in vector space. Word, sentence and document embeddings all exist. Because meaning is encoded as position, you can measure semantic similarity mathematically, typically with cosine similarity, the angle between two vectors. 'How do I reset my password?' and 'I forgot my login details' share few words but have similar embeddings.",
   "Embeddings enable semantic search (finding documents by meaning rather than exact keywords), recommendations, clustering of similar texts and the retrieval step in retrieval augmented generation. Azure OpenAI offers embeddings models for this purpose, and Azure AI Search can store vectors and run vector searches.",
   "A language model, in general terms, is a model that has learned the statistical patterns of a language: which tokens tend to follow which, and what they mean in context. Tokens and embeddings are the foundation on which both traditional NLP features and generative AI are built."
  ],
  terms: [["Tokenization", "Splitting text into tokens such as words or subword pieces for a model to process."], ["TF-IDF", "A weighting that scores words by how frequent they are in a document and how rare across the collection."], ["Embedding", "A vector that represents the meaning of text so similar meanings are close together."], ["Semantic similarity", "How close two texts are in meaning, often measured with cosine similarity between embeddings."]],
  example: "An IT help desk converts every past ticket and knowledge article into embeddings. When a user types 'my laptop will not connect to the office network', semantic search finds the article titled 'Troubleshooting Wi-Fi authentication failures' even though the words barely overlap.",
  tip: "Counting words cannot tell that 'car' and 'automobile' are related; embeddings can. Semantic search and similarity questions point to embeddings.",
  check: [["What is the purpose of tokenization?", "To split text into units (tokens) that can be mapped to numbers and processed by a model."], ["Why can embeddings find related documents that share no words?", "Because they represent meaning as vectors, and texts with similar meaning have vectors that are close together."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Summarization and custom text classification in Azure AI Language",
  body: [
   "Beyond the prebuilt analysis features, Azure AI Language can condense long text into summaries and can learn your own categories for classifying text. These two capabilities appear in scenarios about handling large volumes of documents and messages.",
   "Summarization produces a shorter version of a document or conversation that keeps the key points. Extractive summarization selects the most important sentences from the original text and returns them, each with a rank score, so the summary uses the author's exact words. Abstractive summarization generates new sentences that capture the main ideas, which usually reads more naturally, like a person writing a summary. Conversation summarization is designed for transcripts such as support calls or meetings and can produce summaries of the issue and resolution, or chapter titles for a long recording.",
   "Summarization helps people deal with information overload: getting the gist of a long report, briefing an agent on a customer's previous calls, or creating a short preview for each article. It complements key phrase extraction, which returns separate phrases rather than readable sentences.",
   "Custom text classification lets you train a model to assign your own labels to text. You define the classes, such as 'complaint', 'refund', 'order change' and 'feedback', upload example documents to a storage account, label them in Language Studio, train the model, review its evaluation metrics (precision, recall and F1 per class), and deploy it. Single-label classification assigns one class per document; multi-label classification can assign several, such as a movie synopsis labeled both 'comedy' and 'romance'.",
   "Similarly, custom named entity recognition lets you train the service to extract your own entity types, such as contract clause types or internal part numbers, when the prebuilt entity categories do not cover them.",
   "The pattern matches the rest of Azure AI: use prebuilt features first when they fit, and train a custom model only when your categories or entities are specific to your business. Customizing a model this way needs only labeled examples, not data science expertise. For open-ended summaries or rewriting in a specific style, a generative AI model is another option you will study in domain 5."
  ],
  terms: [["Extractive summarization", "Summarizing by selecting and returning the most important sentences from the original text."], ["Abstractive summarization", "Summarizing by generating new sentences that capture the main ideas."], ["Custom text classification", "Training Azure AI Language to assign your own categories to documents using labeled examples."], ["Multi-label classification", "Classification in which one document can receive more than one label."]],
  example: "A council receives thousands of emails a week. A custom text classification model sorts them into 'waste collection', 'parking', 'council tax' and 'planning' for the right teams, and abstractive summarization gives each officer a two-sentence summary of long email threads.",
  tip: "Short version of a long text: summarization (extractive picks sentences, abstractive writes new ones). Your own categories: custom text classification trained on labeled examples.",
  check: [["What is the difference between extractive and abstractive summarization?", "Extractive returns key sentences from the original text; abstractive generates new sentences that capture the meaning."], ["What do you need to train a custom text classification model?", "Example documents labeled with your own classes, uploaded to storage and labeled in Language Studio."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Speech recognition (speech to text) and speech synthesis (text to speech) with Azure AI Speech",
  body: [
   "Azure AI Speech is the Azure AI service for working with spoken language. Its two core capabilities are speech recognition, which turns audio into text, and speech synthesis, which turns text into audio. Many voice solutions use both.",
   "Speech to text (speech recognition) converts spoken audio into written text. The service uses an acoustic model that maps audio signals to sounds (phonemes) and a language model that maps sounds to the most likely words and sentences. It can transcribe in real time from a microphone or stream, which suits live captions and voice commands, or in batch from stored audio files, which suits transcribing recorded calls or meetings at scale. Output can include punctuation, timestamps and, with diarization, which speaker said what.",
   "When your audio includes specialist vocabulary (product names, medical terms) or challenging acoustic conditions, you can improve accuracy with custom speech by providing sample audio and text to adapt the model. Typical scenarios: meeting transcripts, captioning videos, call centre analytics, dictation and voice-controlled apps.",
   "Text to speech (speech synthesis) converts text into spoken audio. Azure AI Speech offers a large range of neural voices across many languages and locales, which sound natural and handle intonation well. You can control pronunciation, speaking rate, pitch, pauses and speaking style with Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML), an XML-based markup. Typical scenarios: voice assistants, reading content aloud for accessibility, audio announcements and generating narration. Custom neural voice, which creates a voice resembling a specific person, is a Limited Access feature.",
   "Speech also offers speech translation (covered with Translator), speaker recognition and other capabilities. You create an Azure AI Speech resource (single-service, with a free F0 tier where available, or through a multi-service resource) and try features in the speech playground in the Foundry portal or Speech Studio before using the SDK or REST API.",
   "The exam trap is direction. Audio in, text out is speech to text. Text in, audio out is text to speech. A voice bot usually needs both: speech to text to hear the user and text to speech to reply."
  ],
  terms: [["Speech to text", "Converting spoken audio into written text; also called speech recognition."], ["Text to speech", "Converting text into spoken audio; also called speech synthesis."], ["Neural voice", "A natural-sounding synthetic voice produced by a deep learning model."], ["SSML", "Speech Synthesis Markup Language, used to control pronunciation, rate, pitch and style of synthesized speech."]],
  example: "A bank's phone line uses speech to text to understand callers who say 'check my balance', looks up the balance, and replies with text to speech in a neural voice. Recorded calls are transcribed overnight in batch for quality review, with custom speech trained on the bank's product names.",
  tip: "Always check the direction: captions and transcripts are speech to text; reading text aloud is text to speech. Custom neural voice needs Limited Access approval.",
  check: [["A podcast host wants written transcripts of every episode. Which capability?", "Speech to text (speech recognition), transcribing the audio files in batch."], ["What is SSML used for?", "To control how text to speech sounds, such as pronunciation, speed, pitch, pauses and speaking style."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Azure AI Translator for text and documents, and speech translation",
  body: [
   "Translation is one of the oldest AI workloads, and modern neural machine translation has made it good enough for everyday business use. In Azure, text and document translation is provided by Azure AI Translator, while translation of spoken audio is provided by Azure AI Speech.",
   "Early machine translation worked word by word or phrase by phrase, which produced awkward results because languages differ in word order, idioms and grammar. Neural machine translation uses deep learning models that consider the whole sentence and its context, producing much more fluent and accurate output. That is what Azure AI Translator uses.",
   "Azure AI Translator's text translation translates text between a large number of supported languages. A single request can translate into several target languages at once. If you do not specify the source language, the service detects it and returns the detected language with the translation. Transliteration converts text from one script to another, such as from Cyrillic characters to Latin characters, without translating the meaning. A dictionary lookup returns alternative translations for a word.",
   "You can control output in useful ways. A profanity filter can mark or remove profanity in translations. You can mark text that must not be translated, such as product names, and custom translation lets you train the service on your own parallel texts so domain terms are translated consistently, for example in legal or engineering content.",
   "Document translation translates whole files, such as Word, PDF, PowerPoint and HTML documents, while preserving their structure and formatting. It works asynchronously on documents stored in Azure Blob Storage and writes the translated files back to storage, which suits translating manuals, contracts and policies at scale.",
   "Speech translation in Azure AI Speech takes spoken audio in one language and returns translated text, and optionally synthesized speech, in another language in near real time. It combines speech recognition, translation and optionally speech synthesis in one step. Typical uses are live subtitles in another language at events, multilingual meetings and travel apps. For AI-900: written text or files, use Translator; spoken audio, use speech translation in Azure AI Speech."
  ],
  terms: [["Neural machine translation", "Translation by deep learning models that consider the whole sentence and its context."], ["Transliteration", "Converting text from one writing script to another without translating its meaning."], ["Document translation", "Translating whole files while preserving their structure and formatting."], ["Speech translation", "Translating spoken audio into text or speech in another language in near real time."]],
  example: "A travel company uses Translator to publish its website in eight languages from one request per page, document translation to convert its 60-page booking terms PDF into Spanish with formatting intact, and speech translation so tour guides' spoken English appears as Japanese subtitles on visitors' phones.",
  tip: "Text or documents: Azure AI Translator. Spoken audio: speech translation in Azure AI Speech. Converting scripts without changing meaning: transliteration.",
  check: [["What does Translator return if you omit the source language?", "It detects the source language and returns it along with the translation."], ["Which capability preserves formatting when translating a Word document?", "Document translation in Azure AI Translator."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Conversational language understanding: utterances, intents and entities",
  body: [
   "When people talk to an app in their own words, the app must work out what they want and pull out the details it needs. Conversational language understanding (CLU), a feature of Azure AI Language, does this. It is the successor to the older Language Understanding (LUIS) service, which was retired.",
   "CLU is built on three concepts. An utterance is something a user might say or type, such as 'Book a table for four at 7pm tomorrow' or 'What's the weather like in Madrid?'. An intent is the goal or action the user wants, such as BookTable or GetWeather. An entity is a piece of information in the utterance that the app needs to act, such as the party size (four), the time (7pm tomorrow) or the location (Madrid).",
   "To build a CLU model, you create a project in Language Studio, define the intents and entities your app cares about, and add example utterances for each intent, labeling the entities in them. Good practice includes many varied utterances per intent and a None intent for things the app should not handle. You then train the model, review its evaluation results, and deploy it. Entities can be learned from labeled examples, or defined as lists of known values or with prebuilt components for common types like numbers and dates.",
   "At runtime, your app sends the user's utterance to the deployed model. The response contains the top intent with a confidence score and the entities it found. The app then acts: calling a booking system with the extracted values, asking a follow-up question if an entity is missing, or passing the conversation elsewhere if confidence is low.",
   "CLU does not produce the reply text itself and does not look up answers from a knowledge base. It turns natural language into structured data (intent plus entities) that your code acts on. It is commonly used inside bots, voice assistants and apps with a natural language command box, often combined with speech to text for voice input.",
   "Know the vocabulary precisely: utterance is the input, intent is the goal, entity is a detail. Exam questions often give a sentence and ask which part is which."
  ],
  terms: [["Utterance", "An example of something a user might say or type to an app."], ["Intent", "The goal or action a user wants, predicted from an utterance."], ["Entity", "A specific detail in an utterance that the app needs, such as a date, place or quantity."], ["Conversational language understanding (CLU)", "An Azure AI Language feature that predicts intents and extracts entities from user input."]],
  example: "A smart home app receives 'Turn off the kitchen lights in ten minutes'. CLU returns the intent TurnOff with 0.96 confidence and the entities device = lights, room = kitchen and time = in ten minutes. The app schedules the action through the home automation API.",
  tip: "In 'Order two large pizzas', the whole sentence is the utterance, OrderPizza is the intent, and 'two' and 'large' are entities. CLU returns structured intent and entities, not an answer.",
  check: [["In 'Cancel my 3pm meeting with Sam', identify the intent and entities.", "The intent is CancelMeeting; the entities are 3pm (time) and Sam (person)."], ["Why add a None intent to a CLU project?", "To catch utterances the app should not handle, so they are not forced into a wrong intent."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Question answering: building a knowledge base from FAQs for a bot",
  body: [
   "Many organizations already have the answers customers need, spread across FAQ pages, product manuals and help articles. Question answering, a feature of Azure AI Language, turns that content into a knowledge base that a bot or app can query in natural language. It replaced the older QnA Maker service.",
   "A question answering project holds question and answer pairs. You can import them from existing sources, such as FAQ web pages, documents (PDF, Word and similar files) or structured files, and the service extracts question and answer pairs automatically. You can also add pairs by hand, and add alternative phrasings of the same question so the knowledge base recognizes different ways people ask.",
   "When a user asks a question, the service uses natural language understanding to find the best matching question in the knowledge base, even when the wording differs, and returns the associated answer with a confidence score. If no answer is confident enough, the app can return a default response, such as offering a human agent.",
   "Several features make the experience conversational. Chit-chat adds prebuilt answers to small talk, such as 'hello' or 'who are you?', in a chosen personality, for example professional or friendly. Multi-turn conversations use follow-up prompts to guide users through a topic, such as asking which product they have before giving troubleshooting steps. Active learning suggests alternative questions based on real user queries so you can improve the knowledge base over time.",
   "You build and test the project in Language Studio, then deploy it. Bots are commonly created with Azure AI Bot Service, which connects the knowledge base to channels such as a website chat window, Microsoft Teams or other messaging platforms.",
   "The key distinction from conversational language understanding: question answering returns an answer from stored content; CLU identifies an intent and entities so your code can take an action. A support bot might use both, and generative AI with retrieval augmented generation is a newer approach to the same problem, covered in domain 5."
  ],
  terms: [["Question answering", "An Azure AI Language feature that answers natural language questions from a knowledge base of question and answer pairs."], ["Knowledge base", "The collection of question and answer pairs, often imported from FAQs and documents, that question answering searches."], ["Chit-chat", "Prebuilt responses to small talk that give a bot a consistent personality."], ["Multi-turn conversation", "Follow-up prompts that guide a user through several steps within a topic."]],
  example: "A university imports its admissions FAQ page and student handbook into a question answering project, adds chit-chat with a friendly personality, and publishes the bot to its website with Azure AI Bot Service. Students asking 'when do applications close?' or 'what's the deadline to apply?' get the same answer.",
  tip: "Existing FAQ content and 'answer common questions' points to question answering. 'Understand what the user wants to do and extract details' points to conversational language understanding.",
  check: [["How can you build a question answering knowledge base quickly?", "Import existing FAQ pages or documents; the service extracts question and answer pairs automatically."], ["What does chit-chat add to a knowledge base?", "Prebuilt answers to small talk in a chosen personality."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Choosing the right Azure language or speech service for a scenario",
  body: [
   "Domain 4 questions often describe a need and ask which Azure service or feature to use. As with vision, the reliable method is to identify the input (text or audio) and the output the scenario wants, then map them. This lesson pulls the domain together as a decision guide.",
   "If the input is text and the output is information about that text, use Azure AI Language. Choose the feature by the output: which language (language detection), positive or negative (sentiment analysis), sentiment about specific aspects (opinion mining), main topics (key phrase extraction), people, places, dates and other categories (named entity recognition), which real-world entity is meant (entity linking), personal data found and masked (PII detection), a shorter version (summarization) or your own categories (custom text classification).",
   "If the input is a user's request and the output is an action, use conversational language understanding to get the intent and entities. If the output is an answer from existing FAQ content, use question answering. If the output needs to be freely written text, such as a detailed personalized reply, a generative AI model may be the better fit.",
   "If the input or output is audio, use Azure AI Speech: speech to text for transcripts and captions, text to speech for spoken output, and speech translation for spoken audio in another language. If the input is text or documents that need to be in another language, use Azure AI Translator: text translation, or document translation to keep formatting.",
   "Real solutions chain services together. A multilingual voice bot might use speech to text, Translator, CLU or question answering, then text to speech. A call analytics pipeline might use batch speech to text, PII detection to redact card numbers, sentiment analysis and summarization.",
   "Watch for distractors that sound plausible but do a different job: key phrase extraction does not judge tone; sentiment analysis does not translate; Translator does not handle live audio (speech translation does); question answering does not extract entities for an action."
  ],
  terms: [["Azure AI Language", "Service for analyzing and understanding text, including CLU and question answering."], ["Azure AI Speech", "Service for speech to text, text to speech and speech translation."], ["Azure AI Translator", "Service for translating text and documents between languages."], ["Service chaining", "Combining several AI services in sequence so one's output becomes the next one's input."]],
  example: "An airline's contact centre transcribes calls with speech to text, redacts card numbers with PII detection, scores sentiment and summarizes each call with Azure AI Language, translates summaries for regional teams with Translator, and runs a website bot on question answering for baggage FAQs.",
  tip: "Text analysis: Language. Audio: Speech. Different language: Translator, unless the input is spoken, then speech translation. Action from a command: CLU. Answer from FAQs: question answering.",
  check: [["A company wants spoken customer calls converted to text and then checked for negative sentiment. Which services?", "Azure AI Speech speech to text, then Azure AI Language sentiment analysis."], ["A website must translate its HTML help pages while keeping their layout. Which capability?", "Document translation in Azure AI Translator."]]
 },
 {
  t: "How large language models generate text: tokens, next-token prediction and pretraining",
  body: [
   "Large language models (LLMs) are the engines behind most generative AI. They are transformer-based neural networks with billions of parameters, trained on enormous amounts of text. Understanding how they produce text explains both what they are good at and why they sometimes get things wrong.",
   "An LLM starts by tokenizing the prompt: splitting it into tokens, which may be words, parts of words or punctuation. Each token becomes an embedding vector, and the transformer layers use attention to build a representation of the whole prompt in context. The model then outputs a probability for every token in its vocabulary being the next one.",
   "Generation is next-token prediction repeated. The model picks a token from those probabilities (the choice is influenced by settings such as temperature), appends it to the text and predicts again, one token at a time, until it produces a stop signal or reaches the maximum length. A long answer is therefore many small predictions, each based on everything before it, including the model's own earlier output.",
   "The model learns those probabilities during pretraining, a self-supervised process in which it reads vast amounts of text and learns to predict the next token. Doing this at huge scale teaches it grammar, facts, writing styles and patterns of reasoning. Many models are then further trained on instruction-following examples and human feedback so they behave as helpful assistants, respond to instructions and avoid harmful output. Organizations can also fine-tune some models on their own examples to specialize them, though prompting and grounding are usually tried first.",
   "This design has consequences. A model knows only what was in its training data, up to a cutoff date, and nothing about your private documents unless you provide them in the prompt. It produces what is statistically plausible rather than looking facts up, so it can state false information fluently (often called hallucination). And each request has a limit on tokens (the context window) covering both the prompt and the response. Services typically bill by the number of input and output tokens.",
   "Small language models (SLMs) apply the same ideas at smaller scale; they are cheaper and faster and can run on devices, with less broad capability. Choosing a model is a trade-off between capability, speed and cost."
  ],
  terms: [["Large language model (LLM)", "A very large transformer model trained on massive text data that generates language by predicting tokens."], ["Next-token prediction", "Generating text by repeatedly predicting a likely next token and appending it."], ["Pretraining", "Initial self-supervised training on large text collections that teaches a model language patterns and knowledge."], ["Context window", "The maximum number of tokens a model can handle in one request, covering prompt and response."]],
  example: "Asked 'Write a haiku about autumn rain', a model tokenizes the prompt, predicts 'Soft', then 'drops', then 'on', and so on, each time choosing among likely tokens given everything so far. Asked about a company policy it never saw, it may invent a plausible answer, which is why grounding matters.",
  tip: "LLMs predict tokens; they do not look up stored answers. Anything about knowledge limits, cutoff dates or fabricated facts links back to this, and the usual fix is grounding with your own data.",
  check: [["Why might an LLM give a confident but wrong answer?", "It generates statistically plausible tokens rather than retrieving verified facts, and may lack the relevant information in its training data."], ["What counts toward a model's context window?", "The tokens in the prompt, including system message and any added data, plus the tokens in the response."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Common generative AI scenarios: copilots, chat assistants, content drafting, summarization, code and image generation",
  body: [
   "Generative AI is used across nearly every industry, but most applications fall into a handful of scenario types. AI-900 asks you to recognize them and to see where generative AI fits better or worse than traditional AI services.",
   "Chat assistants answer questions and hold multi-turn conversations in natural language: customer support bots, internal helpdesk assistants and general-purpose assistants. When grounded in company data, they can answer questions about products, policies or procedures. Copilots are assistants embedded in the tools people already use, helping with tasks in context: drafting a document in a word processor, summarizing an email thread, suggesting a formula in a spreadsheet or writing code in an editor. The user stays in control and reviews the output.",
   "Content creation and drafting covers marketing copy, product descriptions, emails, reports, job adverts and social media posts. Generative AI produces a first draft quickly, in a specified tone and format, and a person edits it. Summarization condenses long documents, meeting transcripts or conversation histories into short summaries, action items or bullet points. Transformation tasks rewrite content: changing tone, simplifying for a different reading level, translating or converting notes into a structured format.",
   "Code generation writes, explains, reviews and converts code from natural language descriptions, and can generate tests or documentation. Image generation creates new images from text prompts, useful for concept art, marketing visuals and design exploration. Multimodal models can also accept images as input, so they can describe a chart, answer questions about a photo or extract information from a screenshot.",
   "Agents extend these scenarios by taking actions: an agent can look up data, call business systems and complete multi-step tasks, such as processing a return from start to finish.",
   "Generative AI is not always the best tool. When you need a fixed label, a precise field extraction, deterministic results or low cost at very high volume, a traditional AI service (such as sentiment analysis, Document Intelligence or a trained classifier) is often more accurate and predictable. Generative AI shines when the output is open-ended language or content."
  ],
  terms: [["Chat assistant", "A generative AI app that converses with users in natural language over multiple turns."], ["Copilot", "A generative AI assistant embedded in an app to help users with tasks while they stay in control."], ["Summarization", "Condensing long content into a shorter version that keeps the key points."], ["Code generation", "Using a model to write, explain or convert code from natural language descriptions."]],
  example: "A marketing team uses one model deployment for several tasks: drafting product descriptions from specification sheets, summarizing customer interview transcripts, rewriting copy for different audiences, and generating mood-board images for a campaign, with each output reviewed by a person before use.",
  tip: "Create, draft, rewrite or converse freely: generative AI. Extract a specific field or assign a fixed label reliably: a traditional AI service may be the better answer.",
  check: [["What distinguishes a copilot from a stand-alone chat assistant?", "A copilot is embedded in an app and helps with tasks in that context, with the user in control."], ["Give one case where a traditional AI service is a better fit than generative AI.", "Extracting invoice totals reliably, where Document Intelligence's prebuilt model gives structured, consistent fields."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Prompt engineering: system messages, user prompts, few-shot examples and clear instructions",
  body: [
   "A prompt is everything you send a generative model to get a response. Prompt engineering is the practice of designing prompts that produce accurate, relevant and consistently formatted output. It is the cheapest and fastest way to improve a generative AI app, before considering grounding or fine-tuning.",
   "Chat models receive messages with roles. The system message comes first and sets the model's behavior for the whole conversation: its role and persona, the task, rules and boundaries, tone and output format. For example: 'You are a support assistant for Contoso Outdoor. Answer only questions about Contoso products. If you don't know, say so. Reply in no more than three bullet points.' The user message contains the user's actual request. Assistant messages hold the model's earlier replies, so the conversation history gives context for follow-up questions.",
   "Clear instructions matter. Be specific about what you want, the audience, the length and the format (a table, JSON, bullet points). Break complex tasks into steps. Provide the relevant context rather than assuming the model knows it. Say what to do rather than only what not to do. Use delimiters, such as triple quotes or headings, to separate instructions from content the model should work on.",
   "Few-shot prompting includes a small number of examples of input and desired output in the prompt, so the model copies the pattern: for example, three product reviews each followed by the category you want. Zero-shot prompting gives only the instruction with no examples. One-shot gives a single example. Examples are especially effective for enforcing a format or a style.",
   "Other techniques include asking the model to work through a problem step by step before answering (chain-of-thought style prompting), asking it to cite which provided source supports each statement, and specifying a fallback answer when information is missing. Prompts are also a safety layer: a well-written system message can reduce off-topic, ungrounded or harmful responses, though it does not replace content filters.",
   "Prompt engineering is iterative: try a prompt, inspect results, adjust and test again, ideally with a set of representative test inputs. In Microsoft Foundry, the chat playground lets you edit the system message, add examples and compare results without writing code."
  ],
  terms: [["System message", "Instructions sent before the conversation that set the model's role, rules, tone and output format."], ["User prompt", "The user's request or question sent to the model."], ["Few-shot prompting", "Including a few examples of input and desired output in the prompt so the model follows the pattern."], ["Zero-shot prompting", "Giving the model only an instruction, with no examples."]],
  example: "A retailer's classification prompt returned inconsistent labels. The team added a system message listing the five allowed categories, asked for output as a single word, and included three labeled examples (few-shot). Consistency improved immediately, with no retraining.",
  tip: "Role, rules and format that apply to the whole conversation go in the system message. Examples in the prompt are few-shot. Changing the model's weights is fine-tuning, not prompt engineering.",
  check: [["Where should you put 'Always answer in formal English and never give legal advice'?", "In the system message, because it sets rules for the whole conversation."], ["What is few-shot prompting?", "Including a few examples of input and desired output in the prompt so the model follows the same pattern."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Grounding and retrieval augmented generation (RAG) with your own data",
  body: [
   "A large language model knows only what was in its training data. It has never seen your company's policies, product catalogue or last week's announcements, and it may confidently invent an answer when asked. Grounding solves this by giving the model relevant, trusted information in the prompt and instructing it to base its answer on that information.",
   "Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) is the standard pattern for grounding at scale. First, your content (documents, web pages, database records) is split into chunks and indexed, usually in a search service such as Azure AI Search. Each chunk is typically converted into an embedding vector with an embeddings model, so it can be found by meaning, often combined with keyword search (hybrid search).",
   "When a user asks a question, the app retrieves the most relevant chunks from the index. It then augments the prompt: it adds those chunks to the system or user message with an instruction such as 'Answer using only the sources below and cite them. If the answer isn't there, say you don't know.' Finally, the model generates a response grounded in the retrieved content. The app can show the sources as citations so users can check them.",
   "RAG has important advantages. Answers reflect current and private information without retraining the model; updating the index updates the answers. It reduces fabricated answers (ungrounded output) and adds transparency through citations. It can respect permissions if the retrieval step only returns documents the user is allowed to see. It is also usually cheaper and faster to set up than fine-tuning.",
   "Fine-tuning is different: it continues training a model on your examples to change its behavior or style. It is useful for teaching a consistent format or tone, but it is not the best way to give a model changing facts, because the knowledge is fixed at training time. For factual, frequently changing data, choose RAG.",
   "In Microsoft Foundry you can add your own data to a chat solution, connect an Azure AI Search index, and test grounded answers in the chat playground. Evaluations can then measure groundedness: whether the answers are actually supported by the retrieved sources."
  ],
  terms: [["Grounding", "Providing relevant trusted information in the prompt so the model bases its answer on it."], ["Retrieval augmented generation (RAG)", "A pattern that retrieves relevant content from your data and adds it to the prompt before the model generates an answer."], ["Vector index", "A search index that stores embeddings so content can be retrieved by semantic similarity."], ["Fine-tuning", "Further training a pretrained model on your own examples to change its behavior or style."]],
  example: "An HR chatbot indexes the employee handbook in Azure AI Search. When an employee asks 'How many days of parental leave do I get?', the app retrieves the leave policy section, adds it to the prompt, and the model answers with a citation to that section. When HR updates the policy, re-indexing updates the answers the same day.",
  tip: "Model must answer from your current or private data without retraining: RAG. Model must adopt a style or format consistently: fine-tuning may help. RAG retrieves first, then generates.",
  check: [["What are the three steps of RAG at question time?", "Retrieve relevant content from the index, augment the prompt with it, and generate a grounded answer."], ["Why is RAG usually preferred over fine-tuning for frequently changing facts?", "Updating the index changes answers immediately, whereas fine-tuned knowledge is fixed until you retrain."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Model settings: temperature, top_p and maximum response length",
  body: [
   "Besides the prompt, you can change how a generative model chooses its words with a few request settings (often called parameters). They are visible in the Microsoft Foundry chat playground and can be set in every API call. AI-900 expects you to know what the common ones do and when to change them.",
   "Recall that at each step the model calculates a probability for every possible next token. Temperature controls how that choice is made. A low temperature (close to 0) makes the model strongly favor the most likely tokens, so responses are focused, consistent and nearly the same each time. A higher temperature flattens the probabilities, so less likely tokens are chosen more often, and responses become more varied and creative, but also more likely to wander or contain errors.",
   "Top_p (also called nucleus sampling) limits the choice to the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p. With top_p of 0.1, only the tokens making up the top 10 percent of probability are considered; with 1, all are. Lower values make output more focused. Both temperature and top_p control randomness, so the usual guidance is to adjust one or the other, not both at once.",
   "Maximum response length (max tokens) caps how many tokens the model may generate in its reply. It controls cost and response size, and helps keep answers short, but if set too low the answer is cut off mid-sentence. Remember that the prompt and response together must fit in the model's context window. Stop sequences are another option: text that, when generated, makes the model stop, useful for structured output.",
   "Choose settings by task. Factual question answering, data extraction, classification and code usually work best with low temperature for reliable, repeatable output. Brainstorming, marketing slogans, stories and varied suggestions benefit from higher temperature. Test changes with the same prompts to see the effect.",
   "Settings do not add knowledge. A low temperature makes the model consistent, not correct: if it lacks the facts, it will be consistently wrong. Grounding and good prompts address accuracy; parameters tune style and variability. Note too that some newer reasoning models do not support every setting, so check the model's documentation."
  ],
  terms: [["Temperature", "A setting that controls randomness in token selection; low is focused and consistent, high is varied and creative."], ["Top_p", "A setting that limits token choices to the most probable set whose combined probability reaches p."], ["Max tokens", "A limit on the number of tokens the model can generate in a response."], ["Stop sequence", "Text that tells the model to stop generating when it is produced."]],
  example: "A company uses one model for two jobs. Its invoice-question assistant runs at temperature 0 so the same question always gets the same careful answer. Its slogan generator runs at temperature 0.9 with a 50-token limit so marketers get a fresh, short list of ideas each time.",
  tip: "Need repeatable, factual output: lower temperature. Need creative variety: raise it. Answers cut off mid-sentence: max tokens is too low. Adjust temperature or top_p, not both.",
  check: [["An app returns different wording each time for the same extraction task. Which setting should you lower?", "Temperature (or top_p), to make token selection more deterministic."], ["Does lowering temperature make a model more accurate about facts it does not know?", "No. It makes output more consistent, not more knowledgeable; grounding is needed for missing facts."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Microsoft Foundry (formerly Azure AI Foundry): hubs and projects, the model catalog and the playgrounds",
  body: [
   "Microsoft Foundry is Microsoft's platform and portal for building generative AI apps and agents on Azure. It was previously called Azure AI Studio and then Azure AI Foundry, and you may still see those names in older training material and exam questions. Product names in this area change often; the concepts below are what matter.",
   "Work in Foundry is organized into projects. A project is a workspace for one solution: it holds your model deployments, agents, data connections, evaluations and files, and lets a team collaborate with shared access. Projects sit on top of Azure resources that provide the models, storage, security and billing. In the current design, a Foundry resource contains projects directly. An older design used a hub resource that held shared settings and connections (such as to Azure AI Search or storage) for several projects; hub-based projects still exist, so recognize both terms.",
   "The model catalog is where you discover and compare models. It includes Azure OpenAI models, other Microsoft models (such as the small Phi family), and models from providers such as Meta, Mistral and others, plus open-source models. Each model has a model card describing its capabilities, intended uses, limitations and deployment options. Benchmarks and leaderboards help compare quality, cost and speed. From the catalog you deploy a model to your project, which gives it an endpoint your app can call. Some models are billed per token as a managed service; others run on compute you provision.",
   "The playgrounds let you try things without code. The chat playground lets you pick a deployed chat model, write a system message, add examples, adjust parameters such as temperature and max tokens, add your own data for grounding and see the results immediately. There are also playgrounds for agents, images and some Azure AI services such as speech and language. When a prompt works well, you can view sample code to use it in an application.",
   "Foundry also provides tools for the rest of the lifecycle: connecting data and Azure AI Search for RAG, building agents, running evaluations, configuring content filters and guardrails, and monitoring deployed apps. Azure AI services such as Vision, Language, Speech and Content Safety can be used from the same portal.",
   "For AI-900, remember what each part is for: projects organize the work, the model catalog helps you choose and deploy models, and the playgrounds let you experiment before writing code."
  ],
  terms: [["Microsoft Foundry", "Microsoft's platform and portal for building generative AI apps and agents, formerly Azure AI Studio and Azure AI Foundry."], ["Project", "A Foundry workspace that holds a solution's model deployments, agents, data connections and evaluations."], ["Model catalog", "The Foundry library for discovering, comparing and deploying models from Microsoft, OpenAI and other providers."], ["Model card", "Documentation describing a model's capabilities, intended uses, limitations and deployment options."]],
  example: "A developer creates a Foundry project for an internal policy assistant, compares two chat models in the model catalog using their model cards and benchmarks, deploys the cheaper one, and iterates on the system message in the chat playground before copying the sample code into her app.",
  tip: "Compare and choose models: model catalog. Try prompts and settings without code: playground. Organize a solution's assets and team access: project. Expect both the Azure AI Foundry and Microsoft Foundry names.",
  check: [["What does a model card tell you?", "A model's capabilities, intended uses, limitations and deployment options."], ["Where can you test a system message and temperature against a deployed model without code?", "The chat playground in the Foundry portal."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Azure OpenAI models in Foundry: GPT chat models, embeddings models and image generation models",
  body: [
   "Azure OpenAI gives organizations access to OpenAI's models, hosted in Azure. It is available through Microsoft Foundry, where Azure OpenAI models appear in the model catalog alongside models from other providers. What Azure adds is enterprise hosting: Azure identity and access control, private networking options, regional deployment choices, built-in content filtering and Microsoft's data protection commitments.",
   "The models fall into a few families, and AI-900 expects you to match each family to a job. GPT chat (chat completion) models, such as the GPT-4o and later GPT model series, take a conversation of messages and generate a response. They power chat assistants, copilots, summarization, drafting, classification by prompt and code generation. Some accept images as input as well as text (multimodal). Reasoning models spend more computation working through complex problems before answering, which helps with multi-step tasks such as math and planning.",
   "Embeddings models convert text into vectors that represent meaning. They do not generate readable text. They are used for semantic search, recommendations, clustering and, above all, the retrieval step of RAG, where document chunks and user questions are embedded so the most relevant chunks can be found.",
   "Image generation models create images from text descriptions (DALL-E and the newer GPT image models are examples). Use them for marketing visuals, concept art and design ideas; content filters also apply to image prompts and outputs. Audio-capable models handle speech input and output for voice experiences, and Azure AI Speech remains the service for classic speech to text and text to speech.",
   "To use a model, you deploy it in your Foundry resource or project, choosing a deployment type and capacity; your app then calls the deployment's endpoint using the deployment name. Usage is billed, typically per input and output token for language models; there is no free tier for Azure OpenAI, so delete deployments you are not using. Microsoft states that prompts and completions are not used to train OpenAI's or Microsoft's foundation models.",
   "The exam angle is fit: chat and text generation, choose a GPT chat model; search by meaning, choose an embeddings model; create pictures, choose an image generation model."
  ],
  terms: [["Azure OpenAI", "OpenAI models hosted in Azure with Azure security, networking, content filtering and data protection."], ["Chat completion model", "A model that takes a conversation of messages and generates the next response, such as a GPT model."], ["Embeddings model", "A model that converts text into vectors for semantic search and similarity, not readable text."], ["Deployment", "An instance of a model in your resource, with a name and endpoint that your app calls."]],
  example: "A publisher builds a research assistant. An embeddings model vectorizes 20 years of articles for semantic search, a GPT chat model answers readers' questions using the retrieved articles, and an image generation model creates illustrations for newsletters.",
  tip: "Vectors for search and RAG: embeddings model. Conversation and text generation: GPT chat model. Pictures from text: image generation model. Azure OpenAI is billed, with no free tier.",
  check: [["Which model type is used to index documents for semantic search in a RAG solution?", "An embeddings model, which converts text into vectors."], ["What does an app use to call a specific model in Azure OpenAI?", "The endpoint and the name of the model deployment, with a key or Microsoft Entra ID authentication."]]
 },
 {
  t: "AI agents: models with instructions, tools and knowledge, and the Foundry Agent Service",
  body: [
   "An AI agent is a generative AI application that can reason about a goal and act to achieve it, not just reply with text. Agents are one of the fastest-growing parts of generative AI and a named topic in AI-900's generative AI domain.",
   "An agent combines three things with a model. Instructions (similar to a system message) define its purpose, behavior and limits: 'You are an IT support agent. Help employees reset passwords and check ticket status. Never change admin accounts.' Knowledge gives it information to ground its answers, such as files, a search index or web search. Tools let it do things: search, run code to analyze data, call an API, query a database or trigger a workflow. The model decides when to use which tool, calls it, reads the result and continues until the task is done or it needs to ask the user.",
   "That loop of reasoning, acting and observing is what separates an agent from a basic chat assistant. A chat assistant answers 'Your ticket system shows how to reset passwords like this...'. An agent looks up the user, calls the password reset API, confirms success and records it in the ticket. Agents can also work together in multi-agent solutions, where specialist agents handle parts of a larger task and coordinate.",
   "Foundry Agent Service (earlier called Azure AI Agent Service) is the Microsoft Foundry capability for building, deploying and managing agents. You choose a model, write instructions, and attach knowledge and tools, such as file search, Azure AI Search, a code interpreter, Bing grounding or your own functions and APIs, then test the agent in the agents playground. The service manages conversation threads, runs tool calls and handles the plumbing so developers can focus on the agent's behavior. Microsoft also offers agent-building options in products like Copilot Studio.",
   "Agents raise the stakes for responsible AI because they can take real actions. Good practice includes giving each agent only the tools and permissions it needs (least privilege), requiring human approval for consequential actions such as payments or deletions, logging every action for accountability, applying content filters and protecting against prompt injection, where malicious text in a document or web page tries to redirect the agent.",
   "For the exam, look for scenarios where the AI must complete a task using systems or data, not only talk: those describe agents."
  ],
  terms: [["AI agent", "A generative AI application that uses a model with instructions, knowledge and tools to reason and take actions toward a goal."], ["Tool", "A capability an agent can call, such as search, code execution or an API."], ["Foundry Agent Service", "The Microsoft Foundry capability for building, deploying and managing agents."], ["Prompt injection", "Malicious instructions hidden in input content that try to make a model or agent act against its instructions."]],
  example: "A travel company's booking agent reads a customer's request, searches flight availability through an API tool, checks the company's travel policy file for fare rules, holds the best option and asks the employee to confirm before it books and emails the itinerary.",
  tip: "Only answers questions: chat assistant or copilot. Uses tools to take actions or complete multi-step tasks: agent. Agents need least-privilege tools and human approval for high-impact actions.",
  check: [["What three things does an agent combine with a model?", "Instructions, knowledge and tools."], ["Why should an agent that can issue refunds require human approval above a certain amount?", "Agents can take real actions; human oversight limits harm from mistakes or manipulation, supporting reliability and accountability."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Responsible generative AI: identify, measure, mitigate and operate; content filters and Azure AI Content Safety",
  body: [
   "Generative AI brings new kinds of risk: output that is harmful, biased, false or leaks information, and users who try to misuse the system. Microsoft's guidance for responsible generative AI organizes the work into four stages: identify, measure, mitigate and operate. AI-900 tests the stages and the Azure tools that support them.",
   "Identify potential harms. List what could go wrong for this specific solution, such as offensive content, dangerous advice, fabricated facts, privacy leaks or being tricked into ignoring instructions, and prioritize them by likelihood and impact. Red teaming (testing by deliberately trying to provoke failures) helps find them. Document the results.",
   "Measure the harms. Create test prompts that are likely to trigger each harm, run them through the system, and measure how often and how severely harmful output occurs, using manual review and automated evaluations. Measuring gives a baseline to compare mitigations against.",
   "Mitigate the harms in layers. At the model layer, choose a model suited to the use case and, if needed, fine-tune it. At the safety system layer, use content filters and abuse monitoring. At the system message and grounding layer, write instructions that define behavior and ground answers in trusted data. At the user experience layer, design the interface to limit misuse, disclose that content is AI-generated, and encourage users to verify output. Each layer catches what others miss.",
   "Operate responsibly. Before release, complete legal, privacy and security reviews; then roll out gradually, monitor usage and feedback, have an incident response plan and be ready to roll back.",
   "Azure OpenAI deployments include a content filtering system by default. It classifies both prompts and completions into harm categories (hate, sexual, violence and self-harm) at severity levels, and blocks content above the configured threshold. Optional filters detect jailbreak attempts (prompt attacks), protected material such as known copyrighted text or code, and ungrounded content. Azure AI Content Safety is a standalone service that offers the same kinds of analysis for any app: moderating user-generated text and images, detecting prompt attacks and checking groundedness. Filters reduce risk but do not remove it, which is why the other layers matter."
  ],
  terms: [["Identify, measure, mitigate, operate", "Microsoft's four stages for developing and running generative AI responsibly."], ["Content filter", "A safety system that classifies prompts and responses for harmful content and blocks it above a set severity."], ["Azure AI Content Safety", "A service that detects harmful content in text and images and offers features such as prompt attack detection."], ["Jailbreak", "A prompt designed to trick a model into ignoring its instructions or safety rules."]],
  example: "A school builds a homework helper. The team identifies harms (inappropriate content, giving answers instead of guidance), measures them with 500 test prompts, mitigates with strict content filter thresholds, a system message that tells the model to coach rather than solve, and an interface that shows students it is AI, then operates it with teacher feedback and weekly reviews.",
  tip: "Memorize the order: identify, measure, mitigate, operate. Mitigation has four layers: model, safety system, system message and grounding, user experience. Content filters cover hate, sexual, violence and self-harm.",
  check: [["Which mitigation layer does a content filter belong to?", "The safety system layer."], ["What does Azure AI Content Safety offer beyond the built-in Azure OpenAI filters?", "It is a standalone service you can use to moderate any app's text and images, with features such as prompt attack detection and groundedness checks."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Evaluating generative AI apps: groundedness, relevance, fluency and safety evaluations, and red teaming",
  body: [
   "Traditional ML models are evaluated with metrics like accuracy or RMSE against known labels. Generative AI output is open-ended text, so there is rarely one right answer to compare with. Evaluation therefore uses a mix of quality metrics, safety metrics, human review and adversarial testing, and it continues after release.",
   "Quality evaluations judge whether responses are useful. Groundedness measures whether the claims in a response are supported by the provided context, such as the documents retrieved in a RAG app; a low score signals fabricated content. Relevance measures whether the response actually addresses the user's question. Coherence measures whether it is logically organized and easy to follow, and fluency whether the language is grammatically correct and natural. When you have expected answers, similarity metrics compare responses with them. Retrieval quality can also be evaluated, checking whether the right documents were found.",
   "Many of these metrics are calculated with AI-assisted evaluation, in which a capable model acts as a judge and scores each response against a rubric. Microsoft Foundry provides built-in evaluators you can run over a test dataset of questions (and, optionally, context and expected answers), then compare results across prompt versions or models. Automated scores should be spot-checked by people, because the judging model can be wrong too.",
   "Safety evaluations measure risk: how often responses contain hateful, sexual, violent or self-harm content, whether the system can be jailbroken, and whether it reproduces protected material. They use sets of adversarial test prompts and report defect rates, supporting the measure stage of responsible generative AI.",
   "Red teaming means people (and sometimes automated tools) deliberately trying to make the system fail: provoking harmful content, extracting its system message, bypassing its rules with prompt injection or getting it to reveal private data. Findings feed back into mitigations. Microsoft recommends red teaming both the base model and the complete application, with its system message, grounding and filters in place.",
   "Evaluation is not one-off. Run the same test sets whenever you change the prompt, model or data, and monitor production traffic and user feedback for new failure modes."
  ],
  terms: [["Groundedness", "How well a response's claims are supported by the provided source context."], ["Relevance", "How well a response addresses the user's question."], ["AI-assisted evaluation", "Using a model as a judge to score responses against criteria such as groundedness or coherence."], ["Red teaming", "Deliberately probing an AI system to find harmful outputs and weaknesses before attackers or users do."]],
  example: "Before launching a benefits chatbot, a team runs 300 test questions through Foundry evaluations. Groundedness averages 3.1 out of 5, revealing answers that go beyond the retrieved documents, so they tighten the system message. A red team session then finds a prompt that extracts the system message, which they mitigate with a prompt attack filter.",
  tip: "Supported by sources: groundedness. Answers the question: relevance. Reads naturally: fluency. Logically organized: coherence. Deliberately trying to break it: red teaming.",
  check: [["A RAG app's answers read well but include facts not in the retrieved documents. Which metric will be low?", "Groundedness."], ["Why should automated AI-assisted scores be spot-checked by people?", "Because the judging model can also make mistakes, so human review confirms the scores are meaningful."]]
 }
], { reviewed: "2026-09-25" });
